export const html = "<header class=\"sitenav\"><div class=\"in\"><a class=\"brand\" href=\"/\" aria-label=\"Concept Lab home\"><img class=\"logo-l\" src=\"/brand/concept-lab-logo-light.svg\" alt=\"Concept Lab\" height=\"32\"><img class=\"logo-d\" src=\"/brand/concept-lab-logo-dark.svg\" alt=\"\" height=\"32\"></a><button type=\"button\" class=\"tt\" data-theme-toggle=\"flip\" aria-label=\"Switch between light and dark mode\"><span class=\"i-sun\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"4\"/><path d=\"M12 2.5v2M12 19.5v2M2.5 12h2M19.5 12h2M5.3 5.3l1.4 1.4M17.3 17.3l1.4 1.4M5.3 18.7l1.4-1.4M17.3 6.7l1.4-1.4\"/></svg></span><span class=\"i-moon\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M20.5 14.2A8.5 8.5 0 0 1 9.8 3.5a8.5 8.5 0 1 0 10.7 10.7z\"/></svg></span></button><nav aria-label=\"Site\"><a href=\"/\">Videos</a><a href=\"/manual\" aria-current=\"page\">Field manual</a><a href=\"/case-study\">Case study</a></nav></div></header>\n<link rel=\"preconnect\" href=\"https://fonts.googleapis.com\">\n\n\n\n<svg width=\"0\" height=\"0\" style=\"position:absolute\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"ar\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0,0L10,5L0,10z\" class=\"mk\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"ara\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0,0L10,5L0,10z\" class=\"mka\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"arb\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0,0L10,5L0,10z\" class=\"mkb\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"arg\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0,0L10,5L0,10z\" class=\"mkg\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n</svg>\n\n<div class=\"wrap\">\n<header class=\"hero\">\n  <p class=\"kicker\">From zero to interview-ready</p>\n  <h1>System Design Field Manual</h1>\n  <p>Twelve building blocks that every system design question is made from. For each one you get a plain-English picture, a diagram, where real companies use it, the actual tools, which option to pick in which situation, what breaks and how teams prevent it, and the questions interviewers ask at each level.</p>\n  <figure class=\"live\" id=\"live\"><div class=\"scroll\">\n  <svg viewBox=\"0 0 760 132\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Live animation of requests flowing from users through a load balancer to servers, cache and database\">\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"10\" y=\"46\" width=\"96\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"58\" y=\"71\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Users</text>\n    <rect class=\"na\" x=\"170\" y=\"46\" width=\"110\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"225\" y=\"71\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Load balancer</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"345\" y=\"6\" width=\"116\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"403\" y=\"28\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Server 1</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"345\" y=\"49\" width=\"116\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"403\" y=\"71\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Server 2</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"345\" y=\"92\" width=\"116\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"403\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Server 3</text>\n    <rect class=\"na\" x=\"540\" y=\"14\" width=\"110\" height=\"36\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"595\" y=\"37\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Redis cache</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"540\" y=\"82\" width=\"110\" height=\"36\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"595\" y=\"105\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Database</text>\n    <rect class=\"no\" x=\"680\" y=\"46\" width=\"72\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"716\" y=\"65\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Kafka</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"716\" y=\"79\" text-anchor=\"middle\">events</text>\n    <path class=\"e\" d=\"M106,66 L167,66\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/>\n    <path class=\"e\" d=\"M280,60 L342,25\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/><path class=\"e\" d=\"M280,66 L342,66\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/><path class=\"e\" d=\"M280,72 L342,108\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/>\n    <path class=\"e\" d=\"M461,40 L537,34\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/><path class=\"e\" d=\"M461,90 L537,98\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/>\n  </svg></div>\n  <p><span class=\"livedot\"></span>Live: requests flow in (blue), most are answered from the cache (green), some go to the database, writes emit events. Every box here is one topic below.</p></figure>\n  <div class=\"howto\">\n    <div><b>Read in order</b>Each topic builds on the one before. Topics 1, 3, 4, 5 and 7 cover most interviews.</div>\n    <div><b>Learn the diagrams</b>In the interview you will draw these same boxes on a whiteboard. Practice redrawing each from memory.</div>\n    <div><b>Memorize the trade-offs</b>Interviewers rarely want \"the right answer\". They want to hear why you chose it and what it costs.</div>\n    <div><b>Finish with the playbook</b>The last section shows how to combine all twelve to answer any question.</div>\n  </div>\n</header>\n\n<nav class=\"rail\" aria-label=\"Topics\"><ul>\n  <li><a href=\"#t1\"><span>01</span>Scaling basics</a></li>\n  <li><a href=\"#t2\"><span>02</span>Networking & APIs</a></li>\n  <li><a href=\"#t3\"><span>03</span>Load balancing</a></li>\n  <li><a href=\"#t4\"><span>04</span>Caching</a></li>\n  <li><a href=\"#t5\"><span>05</span>Databases</a></li>\n  <li><a href=\"#t6\"><span>06</span>Distributed systems</a></li>\n  <li><a href=\"#t7\"><span>07</span>Queues & streams</a></li>\n  <li><a href=\"#t8\"><span>08</span>Architecture</a></li>\n  <li><a href=\"#t9\"><span>09</span>Resilience</a></li>\n  <li><a href=\"#t10\"><span>10</span>Storage & search</a></li>\n  <li><a href=\"#t11\"><span>11</span>Security</a></li>\n  <li><a href=\"#t12\"><span>12</span>Observability</a></li>\n  <li><a href=\"#playbook\"><span>\u25b6</span>Interview playbook</a></li>\n</ul></nav>\n\n<!-- ============ 01 ============ -->\n<section class=\"topic\" id=\"t1\">\n  <header class=\"th\"><span class=\"num\">01</span><div>\n    <p class=\"eyebrow\">Foundations</p>\n    <h2>Scalability, availability and the core trade-offs</h2>\n    <p class=\"lede\">Scalability means your system keeps working well as users grow from 100 to 100 million. Every other topic in this manual is a technique for scaling, so learn this vocabulary first.</p>\n  </div></header>\n  <p class=\"analogy\"><b>Think of it like</b>a dosa stall. When the queue grows you can buy a bigger tawa and hire a faster cook (vertical scaling), or open three more counters with three cooks (horizontal scaling). The bigger tawa has a limit. More counters can keep growing, but now you need someone at the entrance sending people to the shortest line.</p>\n  <figure class=\"dg\"><div class=\"scroll\">\n  <svg viewBox=\"0 0 760 260\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Vertical scaling makes one server bigger; horizontal scaling adds servers behind a load balancer\">\n    <text class=\"tt\" x=\"185\" y=\"24\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Vertical scaling \u00b7 scale up</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"40\" y=\"95\" width=\"100\" height=\"70\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"90\" y=\"126\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Server</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"90\" y=\"145\" text-anchor=\"middle\">4 CPU \u00b7 8 GB</text>\n    <path class=\"ea\" d=\"M145,130 L195,130\" marker-end=\"url(#ara)\"/>\n    <rect class=\"na\" x=\"200\" y=\"60\" width=\"140\" height=\"140\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"270\" y=\"126\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Bigger server</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"270\" y=\"145\" text-anchor=\"middle\">64 CPU \u00b7 512 GB</text>\n    <text class=\"ts\" x=\"190\" y=\"232\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Easy, no code change. Has a hardware ceiling</text>\n    <text class=\"tb\" x=\"190\" y=\"250\" text-anchor=\"middle\">and is a single point of failure</text>\n    <line class=\"sep\" x1=\"380\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"380\" y2=\"250\"/>\n    <text class=\"tt\" x=\"580\" y=\"24\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Horizontal scaling \u00b7 scale out</text>\n    <rect class=\"na\" x=\"410\" y=\"105\" width=\"100\" height=\"50\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"460\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Load</text><text class=\"t\" x=\"460\" y=\"145\" text-anchor=\"middle\">balancer</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"600\" y=\"45\" width=\"120\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"660\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Server 1</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"600\" y=\"110\" width=\"120\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"660\" y=\"135\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Server 2</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"600\" y=\"175\" width=\"120\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"660\" y=\"200\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Server 3</text>\n    <path class=\"e\" d=\"M510,125 L597,67\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/><path class=\"e\" d=\"M510,130 L597,130\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/><path class=\"e\" d=\"M510,135 L597,193\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/>\n    <text class=\"ts\" x=\"570\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Add machines as traffic grows.</text>\n    <text class=\"tg\" x=\"570\" y=\"256\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Servers must be stateless (no session in memory)</text>\n  </svg></div>\n  <figcaption>Real systems do both: scale the database vertically as long as possible, scale the stateless app servers horizontally from day one.</figcaption></figure>\n\n  <div class=\"cols\">\n    <div><h3>The vocabulary you must own</h3><ul>\n      <li><b>Latency</b>: time for one request (e.g. 120 ms). <b>Throughput</b>: requests handled per second (QPS).</li>\n      <li><b>Availability</b>: % of time the system works. 99.9% = 8.7 h down per year; 99.99% = 52 min per year.</li>\n      <li><b>SLA / SLO / SLI</b>: SLI is the measurement (p99 latency), SLO is your internal target (p99 &lt; 200 ms), SLA is the promise in the contract with penalties.</li>\n      <li><b>Single point of failure (SPOF)</b>: any component that, if it dies, takes the whole system down. Remove them with redundancy.</li>\n      <li><b>Stateless vs stateful</b>: a stateless server keeps nothing between requests, so any copy can serve any user. Store sessions in Redis or a JWT.</li>\n      <li><b>p50 / p99</b>: half of requests are faster than p50; 99% faster than p99. Always talk about p99, because the slow tail is what users notice.</li>\n    </ul></div>\n    <div><h3>CAP theorem in plain words</h3><ul>\n      <li>When the network between your servers breaks (a <b>partition</b>), you must choose:</li>\n      <li><b>Consistency (CP)</b>: refuse requests rather than return stale data. Used for bank balances, inventory, bookings.</li>\n      <li><b>Availability (AP)</b>: keep answering even if data may be a little old. Used for likes, feeds, view counts.</li>\n      <li>Partitions always happen in real networks, so it's really a choice between C and A during failure.</li>\n      <li><b>PACELC</b> adds: even with no failure (Else), you trade <b>Latency</b> vs <b>Consistency</b>. Waiting for 3 replicas is safer but slower.</li>\n    </ul></div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div><h3>Back-of-the-envelope numbers to memorize</h3><div class=\"tw\"><table>\n    <tr><th>Fact</th><th>Value</th><th>How you use it</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Seconds in a day</td><td class=\"mono\">~86,400 \u2248 10\u2075</td><td>10 M requests/day \u00f7 10\u2075 \u2248 100 QPS average; peak \u2248 2\u20133\u00d7 average</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Read from memory (RAM)</td><td class=\"mono\">~100 ns</td><td>Why caches are so fast</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Read from SSD</td><td class=\"mono\">~100 \u00b5s</td><td>1000\u00d7 slower than RAM</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Round trip in same data center</td><td class=\"mono\">~0.5 ms</td><td>Network calls between services are cheap but add up</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Round trip India \u2192 US</td><td class=\"mono\">~150\u2013250 ms</td><td>Why we use CDNs and multi-region</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>One app server (simple API)</td><td class=\"mono\">~1k\u201310k QPS</td><td>Decide how many servers you need</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>One Postgres/MySQL box</td><td class=\"mono\">~5k\u201320k QPS</td><td>Beyond this, add caching, replicas, then shards</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Storage per year</td><td class=\"mono\">users \u00d7 items/day \u00d7 size \u00d7 365</td><td>100 M users \u00d7 1 photo \u00d7 200 KB \u2248 20 TB/day</td></tr>\n  </table></div></div>\n\n  <div><h3>What breaks, and how we prevent it</h3><div class=\"fails\">\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">One server dies and the site goes down</p><p class=\"fix\">Run at least 2 of everything across 2+ availability zones. Remove every SPOF.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Traffic spike on sale day (Big Billion Day)</p><p class=\"fix\">Autoscaling groups, pre-warming capacity, caching, queueing writes, rate limiting.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Sessions lost when a user hits a different server</p><p class=\"fix\">Make servers stateless: sessions in Redis or signed JWT tokens.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Whole region goes down (AWS Mumbai outage)</p><p class=\"fix\">Multi-region deployment with DNS failover; replicate data across regions.</p></div>\n  </div></div>\n\n  <div><h3>Interview depth</h3><div class=\"qa\">\n    <div class=\"q\"><p class=\"qq\">Vertical or horizontal scaling: which would you use? <span class=\"lvl\">Beginner</span></p><p>Both. Start vertical because it's simple. Make app servers stateless so they scale horizontally behind a load balancer. The database is the hard part to scale horizontally, so delay sharding until replicas and caching are not enough.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"q\"><p class=\"qq\">Is a payment system CP or AP? A news feed? <span class=\"lvl\">Intermediate</span></p><p>Payments are CP: showing a wrong balance or charging twice is worse than a \"try again\" error. A feed is AP: a post appearing 2 seconds late is fine, but the feed must never be blank. Good answers say a single system often mixes both: the cart is AP, the checkout is CP.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"q\"><p class=\"qq\">Estimate the servers needed for 50 M daily active users. <span class=\"lvl\">Intermediate</span></p><p>50 M users \u00d7 20 requests/day = 1 B requests/day \u00f7 10\u2075 s \u2248 10k QPS average, ~30k peak. At ~2k QPS per server that's 15 servers, so plan ~25 for headroom and zone failure. State assumptions out loud; the method matters more than the number.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"q\"><p class=\"qq\">How do you go from 99.9% to 99.99% availability? <span class=\"lvl\">Senior</span></p><p>Availability of components in series multiplies (0.999 \u00d7 0.999 = 0.998), so remove serial dependencies, add redundancy in parallel, deploy across zones and regions, use canary releases (most outages come from deploys), and set up automatic failover with health checks.</p></div>\n  </div></div>\n  <p class=\"say\"><b>Say this in the interview:</b> \"Let me first estimate scale: users, QPS at peak, read-to-write ratio, and storage growth. That tells me where the bottleneck will be.\"</p>\n</section>\n\n<!-- ============ 02 ============ -->\n<section class=\"topic\" id=\"t2\">\n  <header class=\"th\"><span class=\"num\">02</span><div>\n    <p class=\"eyebrow\">How requests travel</p>\n    <h2>Networking, protocols and API design</h2>\n    <p class=\"lede\">Before any server does work, a request has to find it and talk to it. This topic covers the journey of one click and the styles of API you can expose.</p>\n  </div></header>\n  <p class=\"analogy\"><b>Think of it like</b>posting a letter. DNS is the phone book that turns \"swiggy.com\" into an address. TCP is registered post: guaranteed delivery, in order. UDP is a postcard: fast, may get lost. HTTP is the language written in the letter. TLS is the sealed envelope.</p>\n  <figure class=\"dg\"><div class=\"scroll\">\n  <svg viewBox=\"0 0 780 230\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Journey of a request from browser through DNS, CDN, API gateway, service and database\">\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"10\" y=\"60\" width=\"106\" height=\"62\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"63\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Browser / app</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"63\" y=\"106\" text-anchor=\"middle\">the user</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"140\" y=\"60\" width=\"106\" height=\"62\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"193\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\">DNS</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"193\" y=\"106\" text-anchor=\"middle\">name \u2192 IP</text>\n    <rect class=\"na\" x=\"270\" y=\"60\" width=\"106\" height=\"62\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"323\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\">CDN edge</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"323\" y=\"106\" text-anchor=\"middle\">static files</text>\n    <rect class=\"na\" x=\"400\" y=\"60\" width=\"106\" height=\"62\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"453\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\">API gateway</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"453\" y=\"106\" text-anchor=\"middle\">auth \u00b7 limits</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"530\" y=\"60\" width=\"106\" height=\"62\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"583\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\">App service</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"583\" y=\"106\" text-anchor=\"middle\">business logic</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"660\" y=\"60\" width=\"106\" height=\"62\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"713\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Database</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"713\" y=\"106\" text-anchor=\"middle\">stores data</text>\n    <path class=\"e\" d=\"M116,91 L137,91\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/><path class=\"e\" d=\"M246,91 L267,91\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/><path class=\"e\" d=\"M376,91 L397,91\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/><path class=\"e\" d=\"M506,91 L527,91\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/><path class=\"e\" d=\"M636,91 L657,91\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/>\n    <text class=\"ts\" x=\"193\" y=\"40\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\u2460 find the IP (cached)</text>\n    <text class=\"ts\" x=\"323\" y=\"40\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\u2461 images/JS from edge</text>\n    <text class=\"ts\" x=\"453\" y=\"40\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\u2462 check token, rate</text>\n    <text class=\"ts\" x=\"583\" y=\"40\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\u2463 run the logic</text>\n    <text class=\"ts\" x=\"713\" y=\"40\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\u2464 read / write</text>\n    <rect class=\"nd\" x=\"10\" y=\"150\" width=\"756\" height=\"66\" rx=\"6\"/>\n    <text class=\"t\" x=\"24\" y=\"174\">Wire protocols</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"24\" y=\"196\">HTTPS = HTTP + TLS encryption, over TCP (reliable). HTTP/3 runs over QUIC (UDP-based, faster on mobile).</text>\n    <text class=\"ts\" x=\"24\" y=\"210\">Video calls and games often use UDP directly: a lost packet is better than a late one.</text>\n  </svg></div>\n  <figcaption>When asked \"what happens when you type a URL\", walk through these five hops and name the protocol at each.</figcaption></figure>\n\n  <div><h3>API styles: which one, when</h3><div class=\"tw\"><table>\n    <tr><th>Style</th><th>How it works</th><th>Use it when</th><th>Real example</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>REST</td><td>Resources as URLs, HTTP verbs (GET /orders/42). JSON.</td><td>Public APIs, CRUD apps, default choice</td><td>Razorpay, Stripe, Shopify Admin API</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>GraphQL</td><td>One endpoint; client asks for exactly the fields it needs</td><td>Many screens needing different shapes of data; mobile bandwidth matters</td><td>Shopify Storefront API (Hydrogen), GitHub, Facebook</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>gRPC</td><td>Binary (Protocol Buffers) over HTTP/2, typed contracts</td><td>Fast internal service-to-service calls</td><td>Google, Netflix, Uber internal microservices</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>WebSocket</td><td>One long-lived two-way connection</td><td>Chat, live tracking, multiplayer, collaborative editing</td><td>WhatsApp Web, Swiggy delivery tracking, Figma</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Server-Sent Events</td><td>Server pushes events one way over HTTP</td><td>Live scores, notifications, streaming AI responses</td><td>ChatGPT/Claude token streaming, stock tickers</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Long polling</td><td>Client asks; server holds request until there's news</td><td>Fallback when WebSockets are blocked</td><td>Older chat systems</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Webhooks</td><td>Server calls <em>your</em> URL when an event happens</td><td>Payment success, shipment updates between companies</td><td>Razorpay payment.captured, Shiprocket status</td></tr>\n  </table></div></div>\n\n  <div><h3>Tools & frameworks</h3><div class=\"tw\"><table>\n    <tr><th>Tool</th><th>Category</th><th>What it does for you</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Route 53, Cloudflare DNS</td><td><span class=\"tag\">DNS</span></td><td>Name resolution, geo-routing users to the nearest region, health-checked failover</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Kong, AWS API Gateway, Apigee, Envoy</td><td><span class=\"tag\">API gateway</span></td><td>Single front door: authentication, rate limiting, routing, request logging</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Nginx, HAProxy, Traefik</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Reverse proxy</span></td><td>TLS termination, routing, compression, serving static files</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Express, FastAPI, Spring Boot, NestJS, Go net/http</td><td><span class=\"tag\">API frameworks</span></td><td>Where you write REST endpoints</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Apollo Server, Hasura</td><td><span class=\"tag\">GraphQL</span></td><td>GraphQL schema, resolvers, batching (DataLoader)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Socket.IO, Pusher, Ably, AWS API Gateway WebSockets</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Real-time</span></td><td>Managing thousands of open connections and rooms</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>OpenAPI / Swagger, Protobuf</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Contracts</span></td><td>Document and version the API so clients don't break</td></tr>\n  </table></div></div>\n\n  <div class=\"cols\">\n    <div><h3>Good API design rules</h3><ul>\n      <li><b>Nouns, not verbs</b>: <code>POST /orders</code>, not <code>/createOrder</code>.</li>\n      <li><b>Pagination</b>: cursor-based (<code>?after=abc123</code>) for feeds; offset (<code>?page=3</code>) breaks when new items are inserted.</li>\n      <li><b>Idempotency key</b>: client sends a unique <code>Idempotency-Key</code> header so a retried payment isn't charged twice.</li>\n      <li><b>Versioning</b>: <code>/v1/</code> in the path or a header, so old mobile apps keep working.</li>\n      <li><b>Correct status codes</b>: 200 OK, 201 Created, 400 bad input, 401 not logged in, 403 not allowed, 404, 409 conflict, 429 rate limited, 500/503 server errors.</li>\n    </ul></div>\n    <div><h3>What breaks, and how we prevent it</h3><div class=\"fails\" style=\"grid-template-columns:1fr\">\n      <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Client retries a timed-out payment and pays twice</p><p class=\"fix\">Idempotency keys stored for 24 h; server returns the first result for repeated keys.</p></div>\n      <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Mobile app loads 30 API calls per screen</p><p class=\"fix\">Backend-for-frontend (BFF) or GraphQL to aggregate; HTTP/2 multiplexing.</p></div>\n      <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">1 M WebSocket connections overload servers</p><p class=\"fix\">Dedicated connection servers, sticky routing, Redis pub/sub to fan out messages between them.</p></div>\n    </div></div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div><h3>Interview depth</h3><div class=\"qa\">\n    <div class=\"q\"><p class=\"qq\">What happens when you type swiggy.com and press Enter? <span class=\"lvl\">Beginner</span></p><p>Browser checks its DNS cache, then asks a resolver which returns an IP. It opens a TCP connection (3-way handshake), then a TLS handshake to agree on encryption keys. It sends an HTTP GET. The request hits a CDN or load balancer, goes to an app server, which may query cache and database, and returns HTML. The browser then fetches JS, CSS and images (mostly from the CDN) and renders.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"q\"><p class=\"qq\">How would you build live delivery-partner tracking? <span class=\"lvl\">Intermediate</span></p><p>The rider app sends GPS every 3\u20135 s over HTTP or MQTT to a location service that writes the latest position to Redis. The customer app holds a WebSocket (or SSE) to a gateway that subscribes to that order's location channel and pushes updates. Polling every 5 s also works at small scale and is simpler; mention the trade-off.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"q\"><p class=\"qq\">REST vs gRPC for internal services? <span class=\"lvl\">Senior</span></p><p>gRPC is faster (binary, HTTP/2 multiplexing), has strict typed contracts, and supports streaming, so it suits high-volume internal calls. REST is easier to debug, works in browsers, and is better for public APIs. Many companies use REST/GraphQL at the edge and gRPC inside.</p></div>\n  </div></div>\n  <p class=\"say\"><b>Say this in the interview:</b> \"Let me define the API first: <code>POST /v1/orders</code> with an idempotency key, <code>GET /v1/orders/{id}</code>, and a WebSocket channel for status updates.\"</p>\n</section>\n\n<!-- ============ 03 ============ -->\n<section class=\"topic\" id=\"t3\">\n  <header class=\"th\"><span class=\"num\">03</span><div>\n    <p class=\"eyebrow\">Spreading the traffic</p>\n    <h2>Load balancing</h2>\n    <p class=\"lede\">A load balancer sits in front of many servers and decides which one handles each request. It's what makes horizontal scaling and high availability possible.</p>\n  </div></header>\n  <p class=\"analogy\"><b>Think of it like</b>the security guard at a bank who looks at the counters and sends you to the one with the shortest queue, and who stops sending people to a counter whose clerk has gone on break.</p>\n  <figure class=\"dg\"><div class=\"scroll\">\n  <svg viewBox=\"0 0 760 270\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Users send traffic to a load balancer which routes to healthy servers and skips a failed one\">\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"20\" y=\"40\" width=\"100\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"70\" y=\"65\" text-anchor=\"middle\">User A</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"20\" y=\"110\" width=\"100\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"70\" y=\"135\" text-anchor=\"middle\">User B</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"20\" y=\"180\" width=\"100\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"70\" y=\"205\" text-anchor=\"middle\">User C</text>\n    <path class=\"e\" d=\"M120,60 L227,118\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/><path class=\"e\" d=\"M120,130 L227,130\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/><path class=\"e\" d=\"M120,200 L227,142\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/>\n    <rect class=\"na\" x=\"230\" y=\"95\" width=\"150\" height=\"70\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"305\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Load balancer</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"305\" y=\"144\" text-anchor=\"middle\">least connections</text>\n    <rect class=\"nd\" x=\"250\" y=\"200\" width=\"110\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"ts\" x=\"305\" y=\"222\" text-anchor=\"middle\">standby LB</text>\n    <path class=\"nd\" d=\"M305,165 L305,200\"/>\n    <rect class=\"no\" x=\"520\" y=\"30\" width=\"150\" height=\"46\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"595\" y=\"52\" text-anchor=\"middle\">App server 1</text><text class=\"tg\" x=\"595\" y=\"68\" text-anchor=\"middle\">healthy \u00b7 40 conn</text>\n    <rect class=\"no\" x=\"520\" y=\"107\" width=\"150\" height=\"46\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"595\" y=\"129\" text-anchor=\"middle\">App server 2</text><text class=\"tg\" x=\"595\" y=\"145\" text-anchor=\"middle\">healthy \u00b7 12 conn</text>\n    <rect class=\"nw\" x=\"520\" y=\"184\" width=\"150\" height=\"46\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"595\" y=\"206\" text-anchor=\"middle\">App server 3</text><text class=\"tb\" x=\"595\" y=\"222\" text-anchor=\"middle\">/health \u2192 timeout</text>\n    <path class=\"ea\" d=\"M380,118 L517,55\" marker-end=\"url(#ara)\"/><path class=\"ea\" d=\"M380,130 L517,130\" marker-end=\"url(#ara)\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n    <path class=\"eb\" d=\"M380,142 L517,205\" marker-end=\"url(#arb)\"/>\n    <text class=\"tb\" x=\"440\" y=\"196\">\u2715 removed</text>\n    <text class=\"ts\" x=\"455\" y=\"258\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Server 2 gets the next request (fewest connections). Server 3 is skipped until its health check passes again.</text>\n  </svg></div>\n  <figcaption>A load balancer itself must not be a single point of failure: run a pair (active + standby) or use a managed one that is redundant internally.</figcaption></figure>\n\n  <div class=\"cols\">\n    <div><h3>Layer 4 vs Layer 7</h3><ul>\n      <li><b>L4 (transport)</b>: looks only at IP and port. Very fast, can't read the URL. Good for raw TCP, databases, game servers.</li>\n      <li><b>L7 (application)</b>: reads HTTP: path, headers, cookies. Can route <code>/api/*</code> to API servers and <code>/images/*</code> elsewhere, terminate TLS, do A/B splits.</li>\n      <li>Most web systems use an L7 load balancer at the edge.</li>\n    </ul></div>\n    <div><h3>Routing algorithms</h3><ul>\n      <li><b>Round robin</b>: 1, 2, 3, 1, 2, 3. Fine when servers and requests are similar.</li>\n      <li><b>Weighted round robin</b>: a bigger server gets 2\u00d7 the share.</li>\n      <li><b>Least connections</b>: best when some requests are slow (uploads, reports).</li>\n      <li><b>IP hash / sticky sessions</b>: same user always hits the same server. Needed for WebSockets; avoid otherwise.</li>\n      <li><b>Consistent hashing</b>: maps keys to servers on a ring so adding a server only moves ~1/N keys. Used for caches and sharded data.</li>\n    </ul></div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div><h3>Tools & frameworks</h3><div class=\"tw\"><table>\n    <tr><th>Tool</th><th>Type</th><th>Pick it when</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>AWS ALB</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Managed L7</span></td><td>HTTP/HTTPS apps on AWS; path routing, WebSocket support</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>AWS NLB</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Managed L4</span></td><td>Millions of TCP connections, static IP, very low latency</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>GCP Cloud Load Balancing, Azure Front Door</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Global L7</span></td><td>One anycast IP routing users worldwide to the nearest region</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Nginx</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Self-hosted L7</span></td><td>Most common open-source choice; also reverse proxy and static server</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>HAProxy</td><td><span class=\"tag\">L4 / L7</span></td><td>Very high performance; used by GitHub, Reddit</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Envoy</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Service proxy</span></td><td>Microservices, service mesh (Istio), gRPC load balancing</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Kubernetes Service / Ingress</td><td><span class=\"tag\">In-cluster</span></td><td>Spreading traffic across pods inside Kubernetes</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Cloudflare Load Balancing</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Edge</span></td><td>DNS-level failover across clouds or regions, plus DDoS protection</td></tr>\n  </table></div></div>\n\n  <div><h3>What breaks, and how we prevent it</h3><div class=\"fails\">\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">A server crashes but still receives traffic</p><p class=\"fix\">Active health checks (<code>GET /health</code> every 5 s, remove after 2\u20133 failures) plus passive checks on error rates.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">The load balancer itself fails</p><p class=\"fix\">Active-passive pair with a floating IP (keepalived), or a managed LB spread across zones.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Deploying kills users' in-flight requests</p><p class=\"fix\">Connection draining: stop sending new requests, wait 30 s for existing ones to finish, then stop the server.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Sticky sessions overload one server</p><p class=\"fix\">Store session state externally (Redis) and turn stickiness off.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">A new server gets flooded before its cache is warm</p><p class=\"fix\">Slow start: ramp its weight up over a few minutes.</p></div>\n  </div></div>\n\n  <div><h3>Interview depth</h3><div class=\"qa\">\n    <div class=\"q\"><p class=\"qq\">Why do we need a load balancer? <span class=\"lvl\">Beginner</span></p><p>To spread load across servers (scalability), to stop sending traffic to dead servers (availability), and to give clients one stable address while servers come and go (flexibility for deploys and autoscaling).</p></div>\n    <div class=\"q\"><p class=\"qq\">Explain consistent hashing and why it matters. <span class=\"lvl\">Intermediate</span></p><p>With <code>hash(key) % N</code>, changing N from 4 to 5 remaps ~80% of keys, which empties every cache at once. Consistent hashing places servers and keys on a ring; each key goes to the next server clockwise. Adding a server only takes keys from its neighbour (~1/N). Virtual nodes (each server placed 100\u2013200 times on the ring) keep the load even. Used in DynamoDB, Cassandra, and memcached clients.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"q\"><p class=\"qq\">How would you route users globally? <span class=\"lvl\">Senior</span></p><p>GeoDNS or anycast sends users to the nearest healthy region; within a region, an L7 load balancer spreads across zones; inside, Kubernetes or a service mesh balances across pods. Each layer has health checks, so a failed region is removed from DNS automatically.</p></div>\n  </div></div>\n  <p class=\"say\"><b>Say this in the interview:</b> \"I'll put a redundant L7 load balancer in front of stateless app servers, using least-connections with health checks, so I can autoscale and lose a server without users noticing.\"</p>\n</section>\n\n<!-- ============ 04 ============ -->\n<section class=\"topic\" id=\"t4\">\n  <header class=\"th\"><span class=\"num\">04</span><div>\n    <p class=\"eyebrow\">Speed for free</p>\n    <h2>Caching and CDNs</h2>\n    <p class=\"lede\">A cache keeps a copy of frequently used data in fast memory so you don't recompute it or hit the slow database every time. It's the single biggest performance win in most designs.</p>\n  </div></header>\n  <p class=\"analogy\"><b>Think of it like</b>keeping the 20 spices you use daily on the kitchen counter instead of walking to the storeroom each time. The counter is small, so you keep only what you use most, and you refill when something runs out or goes stale.</p>\n  <figure class=\"dg\"><div class=\"scroll\">\n  <svg viewBox=\"0 0 760 260\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Cache-aside pattern: app checks cache, on miss reads database and stores result in cache\">\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"30\" y=\"100\" width=\"140\" height=\"64\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"100\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\">App server</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"100\" y=\"148\" text-anchor=\"middle\">GET /product/42</text>\n    <rect class=\"na\" x=\"330\" y=\"25\" width=\"160\" height=\"62\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"410\" y=\"52\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Redis cache</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"410\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\">RAM \u00b7 ~1 ms</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"330\" y=\"175\" width=\"160\" height=\"62\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"410\" y=\"202\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Database</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"410\" y=\"222\" text-anchor=\"middle\">disk \u00b7 ~10\u201350 ms</text>\n    <path class=\"ea\" d=\"M170,112 L327,52\" marker-end=\"url(#ara)\"/>\n    <text class=\"ts\" x=\"236\" y=\"68\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\u2460 look up key</text>\n    <path class=\"eg\" d=\"M327,78 L172,132\" marker-end=\"url(#arg)\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 4\"/>\n    <text class=\"tg\" x=\"262\" y=\"126\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\u2461 HIT \u2192 return</text>\n    <path class=\"e\" d=\"M170,152 L327,200\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/>\n    <text class=\"ts\" x=\"236\" y=\"196\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\u2462 MISS \u2192 query DB</text>\n    <path class=\"ea\" d=\"M410,175 L410,90\" marker-end=\"url(#ara)\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 4\"/>\n    <text class=\"ts\" x=\"418\" y=\"137\">\u2463 app saves result,</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"418\" y=\"151\">TTL = 10 min</text>\n    <line class=\"sep\" x1=\"540\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"540\" y2=\"240\"/>\n    <text class=\"tt\" x=\"560\" y=\"44\">Why it matters</text>\n    <text class=\"ts\" x=\"560\" y=\"72\">Hit rate 95% means only</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"560\" y=\"88\">5 in 100 reads reach the DB.</text>\n    <text class=\"ts\" x=\"560\" y=\"116\">DB load drops 20\u00d7, latency</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"560\" y=\"132\">drops from ~30 ms to ~1 ms.</text>\n    <text class=\"tb\" x=\"560\" y=\"164\">Cost: data can be stale</text><text class=\"tb\" x=\"560\" y=\"180\">for up to the TTL.</text>\n  </svg></div>\n  <figcaption>This is <b>cache-aside</b> (lazy loading), the pattern you'll use in 80% of interview answers.</figcaption></figure>\n\n  <div><h3>Where caches live (layers)</h3><div class=\"tw\"><table>\n    <tr><th>Layer</th><th>What it caches</th><th>Example</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Browser</td><td>Images, JS, API responses via Cache-Control headers</td><td>Your site's logo is downloaded once per week</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>CDN edge</td><td>Static files, images, video segments, even whole HTML pages</td><td>Netflix video, Flipkart product images, Shopify storefront assets</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>API gateway / reverse proxy</td><td>Whole responses for public endpoints</td><td>Nginx caching <code>GET /trending</code> for 30 s</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Application (distributed cache)</td><td>DB query results, sessions, computed values</td><td>Redis holding product details, user sessions, leaderboards</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Database</td><td>Buffer pool keeps hot pages in RAM</td><td>Postgres shared_buffers</td></tr>\n  </table></div></div>\n\n  <div><h3>Write strategies: which one, when</h3><div class=\"tw\"><table>\n    <tr><th>Strategy</th><th>How</th><th>Use when</th><th>Downside</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Cache-aside</td><td>App reads cache, on miss reads DB and fills cache. On write: update DB, delete cache key.</td><td>Default for read-heavy data (products, profiles)</td><td>First read is slow; brief staleness</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Read-through</td><td>Cache library loads from DB itself on miss</td><td>You want simpler app code</td><td>Needs a cache that supports loaders</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Write-through</td><td>Write to cache and DB together</td><td>Data read right after writing (user settings)</td><td>Every write is slower</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Write-back (write-behind)</td><td>Write to cache now, flush to DB later in batches</td><td>Very high write volume: view counters, likes, analytics</td><td>Data loss if cache dies before flush</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Write-around</td><td>Write only to DB; cache fills on read</td><td>Data written but rarely re-read (logs)</td><td>Recent writes miss the cache</td></tr>\n  </table></div></div>\n\n  <div><h3>Tools & frameworks</h3><div class=\"tw\"><table>\n    <tr><th>Tool</th><th>Type</th><th>Pick it when</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Redis (ElastiCache, Upstash, Redis Cloud)</td><td><span class=\"tag\">In-memory store</span></td><td>Default. Has data structures (lists, sorted sets for leaderboards, pub/sub, TTL, persistence)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Memcached</td><td><span class=\"tag\">In-memory KV</span></td><td>Pure simple key-value caching, multi-threaded; used by Facebook at huge scale</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Cloudflare, Akamai, AWS CloudFront, Fastly</td><td><span class=\"tag\">CDN</span></td><td>Serving static and media content near users; Fastly for instant purge</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Varnish</td><td><span class=\"tag\">HTTP cache</span></td><td>Caching whole pages in front of a CMS or site</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Caffeine (Java), node-cache, lru-cache</td><td><span class=\"tag\">In-process</span></td><td>Tiny hot data on each server (config, feature flags); fastest, but not shared</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Next.js ISR, React Query / SWR</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Frontend</span></td><td>Static page regeneration; client-side caching of API data</td></tr>\n  </table></div></div>\n\n  <div><h3>What breaks, and how we prevent it</h3><div class=\"fails\">\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Stale data: price changed but users see old price</p><p class=\"fix\">Delete the cache key on every write (not update), short TTLs, or publish change events that invalidate keys.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Cache stampede: a hot key expires and 10,000 requests hit the DB at once</p><p class=\"fix\">Lock so only one request rebuilds the value (others wait), refresh ahead of expiry, add random jitter to TTLs.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Hot key: one celebrity profile gets 1 M reads/s on one Redis node</p><p class=\"fix\">Replicate the key across nodes (key#1\u2026key#10), add a small in-process cache in front.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Cache penetration: requests for IDs that don't exist always miss</p><p class=\"fix\">Cache the \"not found\" result briefly, or use a Bloom filter to reject unknown IDs.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Cache cluster restarts empty and the DB collapses</p><p class=\"fix\">Redis replicas with automatic failover (Sentinel/Cluster), warm the cache before taking traffic, rate limit DB access.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Cache fills up</p><p class=\"fix\">Eviction policy: LRU (least recently used) by default, LFU for stable popularity, TTL on everything.</p></div>\n  </div></div>\n\n  <div><h3>Interview depth</h3><div class=\"qa\">\n    <div class=\"q\"><p class=\"qq\">What would you cache in an e-commerce site? <span class=\"lvl\">Beginner</span></p><p>Product pages and images (CDN plus Redis), category listings, sessions and cart (Redis), search suggestions. Don't cache inventory counts at checkout or payment status, because those must be exact.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"q\"><p class=\"qq\">On update, why delete the cache key instead of updating it? <span class=\"lvl\">Intermediate</span></p><p>Two concurrent writers can update the DB in order A then B but the cache in order B then A, leaving the cache wrong forever. Deleting means the next read loads the true value from the DB. The remaining risk window is tiny; delayed double-delete or change-data-capture closes it.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"q\"><p class=\"qq\">How do you size a cache? <span class=\"lvl\">Intermediate</span></p><p>Use the 80/20 rule: 20% of items get 80% of traffic. If you have 10 M products \u00d7 2 KB = 20 GB, caching the hot 20% needs ~4 GB plus overhead, so one 8\u201316 GB Redis node with a replica fits.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"q\"><p class=\"qq\">How does a CDN know when content changes? <span class=\"lvl\">Senior</span></p><p>Either TTL-based expiry, explicit purge API calls on deploy, or (best) content-hashed file names like <code>app.3f9a2c.js</code>, so new content has a new URL and old files can be cached forever.</p></div>\n  </div></div>\n  <p class=\"say\"><b>Say this in the interview:</b> \"Reads are 100\u00d7 writes here, so I'll put Redis in front of the database with cache-aside, a 5-minute TTL with jitter, and delete-on-write. Static assets go through a CDN.\"</p>\n</section>\n\n<!-- ============ 05 ============ -->\n<section class=\"topic\" id=\"t5\">\n  <header class=\"th\"><span class=\"num\">05</span><div>\n    <p class=\"eyebrow\">Where data lives</p>\n    <h2>Databases: choosing, replicating, sharding</h2>\n    <p class=\"lede\">The database is almost always the bottleneck and the hardest thing to change later. Interviewers spend the most time here: which database, how it's modelled, and how it scales.</p>\n  </div></header>\n  <p class=\"analogy\"><b>Think of it like</b>a library. An <b>index</b> is the catalogue that tells you which shelf a book is on. <b>Replication</b> is printing photocopies of the catalogue so many people can read at once. <b>Sharding</b> is splitting the library into branches by author name A\u2013H, I\u2013P, Q\u2013Z when one building can't hold everything.</p>\n  <figure class=\"dg\"><div class=\"scroll\">\n  <svg viewBox=\"0 0 770 290\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Left: primary database takes writes and replicates to read replicas. Right: shard router splits users across three shards by hash\">\n    <text class=\"tt\" x=\"190\" y=\"20\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Replication \u00b7 scale reads</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"10\" y=\"115\" width=\"90\" height=\"56\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"55\" y=\"140\" text-anchor=\"middle\">App</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"55\" y=\"158\" text-anchor=\"middle\">server</text>\n    <rect class=\"na\" x=\"170\" y=\"40\" width=\"140\" height=\"52\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"240\" y=\"63\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Primary</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"240\" y=\"80\" text-anchor=\"middle\">all writes</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"170\" y=\"135\" width=\"140\" height=\"48\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"240\" y=\"156\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Replica 1</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"240\" y=\"172\" text-anchor=\"middle\">reads</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"170\" y=\"215\" width=\"140\" height=\"48\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"240\" y=\"236\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Replica 2</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"240\" y=\"252\" text-anchor=\"middle\">reads</text>\n    <path class=\"ea\" d=\"M100,130 L167,70\" marker-end=\"url(#ara)\"/><text class=\"ts\" x=\"112\" y=\"90\">writes</text>\n    <path class=\"e\" d=\"M100,150 L167,158\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/><path class=\"e\" d=\"M100,160 L167,235\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/><text class=\"ts\" x=\"108\" y=\"210\">reads</text>\n    <path class=\"nd\" d=\"M310,66 C350,66 350,158 312,158\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/>\n    <path class=\"nd\" d=\"M310,66 C370,66 370,238 312,238\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/>\n    <text class=\"ts\" x=\"360\" y=\"120\">copy</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"360\" y=\"134\">(async,</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"360\" y=\"148\">~ms lag)</text>\n    <line class=\"sep\" x1=\"410\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"410\" y2=\"280\"/>\n    <text class=\"tt\" x=\"590\" y=\"20\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Sharding \u00b7 scale writes & size</text>\n    <rect class=\"na\" x=\"430\" y=\"120\" width=\"130\" height=\"58\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"495\" y=\"144\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Shard router</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"495\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\">hash(user_id) % 3</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"620\" y=\"40\" width=\"140\" height=\"50\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"690\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Shard 0</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"690\" y=\"79\" text-anchor=\"middle\">users where hash=0</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"620\" y=\"125\" width=\"140\" height=\"50\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"690\" y=\"147\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Shard 1</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"690\" y=\"164\" text-anchor=\"middle\">users where hash=1</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"620\" y=\"210\" width=\"140\" height=\"50\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"690\" y=\"232\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Shard 2</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"690\" y=\"249\" text-anchor=\"middle\">users where hash=2</text>\n    <path class=\"e\" d=\"M560,140 L617,66\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/><path class=\"e\" d=\"M560,149 L617,150\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/><path class=\"e\" d=\"M560,158 L617,234\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/>\n    <text class=\"ts\" x=\"495\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\">each shard has its</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"495\" y=\"224\" text-anchor=\"middle\">own replicas too</text>\n  </svg></div>\n  <figcaption>The usual scaling path: one database \u2192 add indexes \u2192 add a cache \u2192 add read replicas \u2192 partition big tables \u2192 shard. Go to the next step only when the previous one is not enough.</figcaption></figure>\n\n  <div><h3>SQL vs NoSQL: which one, when</h3><div class=\"tw\"><table>\n    <tr><th>Type</th><th>Tools</th><th>Best for</th><th>Real example</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Relational (SQL)</td><td>PostgreSQL, MySQL, Aurora, Supabase</td><td>Orders, payments, users: anything with relations and transactions (ACID)</td><td>Razorpay payments, Shopify orders, bank ledgers</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Key-value</td><td>Redis, DynamoDB, etcd</td><td>Lookup by a single key at huge scale: sessions, carts, feature flags</td><td>Amazon cart (DynamoDB)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Document</td><td>MongoDB, Firestore, Couchbase</td><td>Flexible JSON shapes that change often: catalogs, CMS content, user profiles</td><td>Product catalogs with varying attributes</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Wide-column</td><td>Cassandra, ScyllaDB, HBase, Bigtable</td><td>Massive write volume, time-ordered data, always-available</td><td>Discord messages, Netflix viewing history</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Graph</td><td>Neo4j, Amazon Neptune</td><td>Relationships are the query: friends-of-friends, fraud rings, recommendations</td><td>LinkedIn connections, fraud detection</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Time-series</td><td>TimescaleDB, InfluxDB, Prometheus</td><td>Metrics and sensor readings over time</td><td>Server CPU graphs, IoT data</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Search engine</td><td>Elasticsearch, OpenSearch, Typesense, Meilisearch</td><td>Full-text and fuzzy search (see topic 10)</td><td>Product search, log search</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Vector DB</td><td>pgvector, Pinecone, Weaviate, Qdrant</td><td>Similarity search on embeddings for AI features</td><td>Semantic search, RAG chatbots</td></tr>\n  </table></div></div>\n\n  <div class=\"cols\">\n    <div><h3>Core concepts</h3><ul>\n      <li><b>ACID</b>: Atomic (all or nothing), Consistent (rules hold), Isolated (transactions don't see each other's half-done work), Durable (survives crash).</li>\n      <li><b>Index</b>: a sorted lookup structure (B-tree) so <code>WHERE email = ?</code> doesn't scan 100 M rows. Speeds reads, slows writes slightly.</li>\n      <li><b>Composite index</b> on <code>(user_id, created_at)</code> serves \"this user's latest orders\" in one lookup. Column order matters.</li>\n      <li><b>B-tree vs LSM-tree</b>: B-trees (Postgres) are great for reads; LSM-trees (Cassandra, RocksDB) buffer writes in memory and flush sequentially, great for write-heavy loads.</li>\n      <li><b>Normalization</b> avoids duplicate data; <b>denormalization</b> copies data (e.g. store author name on each post) to avoid joins at read time.</li>\n      <li><b>Connection pooling</b> (PgBouncer, RDS Proxy): databases handle only a few hundred connections; a pool shares them across thousands of app requests.</li>\n    </ul></div>\n    <div><h3>Sharding strategies</h3><ul>\n      <li><b>Hash-based</b>: <code>hash(user_id) % N</code>. Even spread; range queries across users are hard. Use consistent hashing so you can add shards.</li>\n      <li><b>Range-based</b>: users A\u2013H on shard 1. Range queries easy; hot spots if one range is popular.</li>\n      <li><b>Geo-based</b>: Indian users in Mumbai, EU users in Frankfurt. Good for latency and data laws.</li>\n      <li><b>Directory-based</b>: a lookup table says which shard holds each tenant. Flexible, but the directory must be highly available.</li>\n      <li><b>Choosing the shard key</b> is the big decision: pick the key most queries filter by (user_id, tenant_id), with high cardinality and even load.</li>\n      <li>Tools: Vitess (MySQL, used by YouTube, Slack), Citus (Postgres), MongoDB and Cassandra shard natively.</li>\n    </ul></div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div><h3>What breaks, and how we prevent it</h3><div class=\"fails\">\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Replication lag: user updates profile, refreshes, sees old data</p><p class=\"fix\">Read-your-writes: read from primary for a few seconds after a user's write, or route that user's reads to primary.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Primary database dies</p><p class=\"fix\">Automatic failover: promote a replica (RDS Multi-AZ, Patroni). Synchronous replica in another zone avoids losing data.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Slow queries as the table grows</p><p class=\"fix\">Add indexes (check with <code>EXPLAIN</code>), avoid <code>SELECT *</code>, fix N+1 queries, archive old rows, partition by date.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Hot shard: one big seller's data overloads one shard</p><p class=\"fix\">Better shard key, split hot tenant to its own shard, add a salt to the key.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Cross-shard queries and joins become slow</p><p class=\"fix\">Shard by the key you join on, denormalize, or keep a separate read model (search index, warehouse) for global queries.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Accidental delete or corrupt data</p><p class=\"fix\">Point-in-time recovery backups, tested restores, soft deletes, and delayed replicas.</p></div>\n  </div></div>\n\n  <div><h3>Interview depth</h3><div class=\"qa\">\n    <div class=\"q\"><p class=\"qq\">SQL or NoSQL for a URL shortener? <span class=\"lvl\">Beginner</span></p><p>It's a simple key \u2192 URL lookup with billions of rows and very high reads, no joins. A key-value store like DynamoDB or Cassandra scales easily. Postgres also works fine up to hundreds of millions of rows. Either is acceptable if you justify it with access patterns.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"q\"><p class=\"qq\">How do you prevent two people booking the same seat? <span class=\"lvl\">Intermediate</span></p><p>Use a transaction with a row lock: <code>SELECT \u2026 FOR UPDATE</code> on the seat, check it's free, update it. Or optimistic locking: <code>UPDATE seats SET status='held', version=v+1 WHERE id=? AND version=v</code>; if 0 rows change, someone else got it. Add a temporary hold with expiry (10 min) in Redis while payment happens.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"q\"><p class=\"qq\">What are isolation levels and which anomalies do they stop? <span class=\"lvl\">Senior</span></p><p>Read Committed (Postgres default) prevents dirty reads. Repeatable Read prevents non-repeatable reads. Serializable prevents write skew and phantoms but is slower and retries more. Pick the lowest level that's correct, and use explicit locks for the critical paths.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"q\"><p class=\"qq\">How would you shard a chat app's messages? <span class=\"lvl\">Senior</span></p><p>Shard by <code>conversation_id</code> so all messages in a chat are on one shard and can be read in order with one query. Within a shard, cluster by <code>(conversation_id, message_id)</code> where message_id is time-sortable (Snowflake ID). This is exactly how Discord stores messages in ScyllaDB.</p></div>\n  </div></div>\n  <p class=\"say\"><b>Say this in the interview:</b> \"Orders need transactions, so Postgres with a primary and two read replicas. I'll index on (user_id, created_at). If writes outgrow one primary, I'll shard by user_id with consistent hashing.\"</p>\n</section>\n\n<!-- ============ 06 ============ -->\n<section class=\"topic\" id=\"t6\">\n  <header class=\"th\"><span class=\"num\">06</span><div>\n    <p class=\"eyebrow\">Many machines, one truth</p>\n    <h2>Consistency and distributed systems</h2>\n    <p class=\"lede\">Once data lives on several machines, they can disagree. This topic is about how machines agree, how strongly they must agree, and how to do a multi-step operation across services without a single database transaction.</p>\n  </div></header>\n  <p class=\"analogy\"><b>Think of it like</b>a family WhatsApp group deciding on dinner. Strong consistency: nobody orders until everyone replies. Eventual consistency: everyone orders on their own and the group sorts it out later. A quorum: order once a majority agrees. A leader: Amma decides and everyone follows.</p>\n  <figure class=\"dg\"><div class=\"scroll\">\n  <svg viewBox=\"0 0 770 270\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Left: write to three replicas succeeds when two acknowledge. Right: saga with compensating actions when inventory fails\">\n    <text class=\"tt\" x=\"180\" y=\"20\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Quorum write \u00b7 N=3, W=2</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"10\" y=\"108\" width=\"90\" height=\"48\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"55\" y=\"129\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Client</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"55\" y=\"146\" text-anchor=\"middle\">write x=5</text>\n    <rect class=\"no\" x=\"190\" y=\"35\" width=\"130\" height=\"48\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"255\" y=\"56\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Node A</text><text class=\"tg\" x=\"255\" y=\"73\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\u2713 ack</text>\n    <rect class=\"no\" x=\"190\" y=\"108\" width=\"130\" height=\"48\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"255\" y=\"129\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Node B</text><text class=\"tg\" x=\"255\" y=\"146\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\u2713 ack</text>\n    <rect class=\"ny\" x=\"190\" y=\"181\" width=\"130\" height=\"48\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"255\" y=\"202\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Node C</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"255\" y=\"219\" text-anchor=\"middle\">slow, catches up later</text>\n    <path class=\"eg\" d=\"M100,122 L187,62\" marker-end=\"url(#arg)\"/><path class=\"eg\" d=\"M100,132 L187,132\" marker-end=\"url(#arg)\"/><path class=\"e\" d=\"M100,142 L187,203\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 4\"/>\n    <text class=\"ts\" x=\"170\" y=\"252\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2 of 3 acked \u2192 success. Reads ask R=2 nodes,</text>\n    <text class=\"tg\" x=\"170\" y=\"266\" text-anchor=\"middle\">R + W &gt; N, so a read always sees the newest write</text>\n    <line class=\"sep\" x1=\"370\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"370\" y2=\"262\"/>\n    <text class=\"tt\" x=\"575\" y=\"20\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Saga \u00b7 steps across services</text>\n    <rect class=\"no\" x=\"390\" y=\"50\" width=\"110\" height=\"50\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"445\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Create order</text><text class=\"tg\" x=\"445\" y=\"89\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\u2713</text>\n    <rect class=\"no\" x=\"525\" y=\"50\" width=\"110\" height=\"50\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"580\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Charge card</text><text class=\"tg\" x=\"580\" y=\"89\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\u2713</text>\n    <rect class=\"nw\" x=\"655\" y=\"50\" width=\"105\" height=\"50\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"707\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Reserve stock</text><text class=\"tb\" x=\"707\" y=\"89\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\u2715 out of stock</text>\n    <path class=\"eg\" d=\"M500,75 L522,75\" marker-end=\"url(#arg)\"/><path class=\"eg\" d=\"M635,75 L652,75\" marker-end=\"url(#arg)\"/>\n    <rect class=\"ny\" x=\"525\" y=\"160\" width=\"110\" height=\"50\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"580\" y=\"182\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Refund card</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"580\" y=\"199\" text-anchor=\"middle\">compensate</text>\n    <rect class=\"ny\" x=\"390\" y=\"160\" width=\"110\" height=\"50\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"445\" y=\"182\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Cancel order</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"445\" y=\"199\" text-anchor=\"middle\">compensate</text>\n    <path class=\"eb\" d=\"M707,100 C707,185 660,185 638,185\" marker-end=\"url(#arb)\"/>\n    <path class=\"eb\" d=\"M525,185 L503,185\" marker-end=\"url(#arb)\"/>\n    <text class=\"ts\" x=\"575\" y=\"245\" text-anchor=\"middle\">No global lock: each step commits locally;</text>\n    <text class=\"ts\" x=\"575\" y=\"259\" text-anchor=\"middle\">failure triggers undo steps in reverse</text>\n  </svg></div>\n  <figcaption>Quorums keep replicas of one piece of data in agreement. Sagas keep several services in agreement over one business operation.</figcaption></figure>\n\n  <div class=\"cols\">\n    <div><h3>Consistency levels (strong \u2192 weak)</h3><ul>\n      <li><b>Linearizable / strong</b>: every read sees the latest write, as if there's one copy. Bank balance, seat booking, leader election.</li>\n      <li><b>Sequential / causal</b>: everyone sees related events in the same order (a reply never shows before the message it answers). Comments, chat.</li>\n      <li><b>Read-your-writes</b>: you always see your own updates, even if others see them a bit later. Profile edits.</li>\n      <li><b>Monotonic reads</b>: once you see a value, you never see an older one. Pin a user to one replica.</li>\n      <li><b>Eventual</b>: replicas converge \"soon\". Like counts, view counts, DNS, product reviews.</li>\n    </ul></div>\n    <div><h3>Key mechanisms</h3><ul>\n      <li><b>Consensus (Raft, Paxos)</b>: a majority of nodes agree on each change and elect a leader. Survives losing a minority (3 nodes tolerate 1 failure, 5 tolerate 2). Powers etcd, ZooKeeper, CockroachDB, Kafka KRaft.</li>\n      <li><b>Leader election</b>: only one node acts as primary or runs the cron job; others take over if its lease expires.</li>\n      <li><b>Two-phase commit (2PC)</b>: coordinator asks all participants \"can you commit?\", then \"commit\". Strong but slow and blocks if the coordinator dies. Rare across microservices.</li>\n      <li><b>Saga</b>: chain of local transactions with compensating undo steps. <i>Orchestrated</i> (central coordinator like Temporal) or <i>choreographed</i> (services react to events).</li>\n      <li><b>Distributed lock</b>: Redis <code>SET key NX PX 30000</code> (Redlock) or etcd/ZooKeeper leases, with fencing tokens so a paused old holder can't write.</li>\n      <li><b>Clocks</b>: machine clocks drift, so use logical clocks (Lamport, vector clocks) or hybrid clocks to order events. Snowflake IDs embed time for sortable IDs.</li>\n      <li><b>Gossip & heartbeats</b>: nodes tell each other who's alive (Cassandra, Consul).</li>\n    </ul></div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div><h3>Tools & frameworks</h3><div class=\"tw\"><table>\n    <tr><th>Tool</th><th>Type</th><th>Pick it when</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>etcd, Apache ZooKeeper, Consul</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Coordination</span></td><td>Leader election, config, service discovery, distributed locks (etcd is Kubernetes' brain)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Temporal, AWS Step Functions, Cadence, Camunda</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Workflow / saga</span></td><td>Long multi-step business processes with retries and compensation (order fulfilment, onboarding)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>CockroachDB, Google Spanner, YugabyteDB, TiDB</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Distributed SQL</span></td><td>You need SQL + strong consistency across regions</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Cassandra, DynamoDB, Riak</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Tunable / eventual</span></td><td>High availability with per-query consistency levels (ONE, QUORUM, ALL)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Redis Redlock, Redisson</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Locks</span></td><td>Simple short-lived locks where rare double execution is tolerable</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Yjs, Automerge</td><td><span class=\"tag\">CRDTs</span></td><td>Collaborative editing that merges offline edits automatically</td></tr>\n  </table></div></div>\n\n  <div><h3>What breaks, and how we prevent it</h3><div class=\"fails\">\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Split brain: network splits and two nodes both think they're leader</p><p class=\"fix\">Majority quorum (need 2 of 3 to lead), leases with timeouts, fencing tokens on writes.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Payment charged but order never created</p><p class=\"fix\">Saga with compensations, plus the outbox pattern (topic 7) so the event is never lost.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Same message processed twice after a retry</p><p class=\"fix\">Idempotent handlers: store processed message IDs, use upserts, unique constraints.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Lock holder pauses (GC), lock expires, two workers write</p><p class=\"fix\">Fencing token: storage rejects writes with an older token number.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Clock skew orders events wrongly</p><p class=\"fix\">Don't rely on wall clocks for ordering; use sequence numbers, logical clocks, or a single sequencer.</p></div>\n  </div></div>\n\n  <div><h3>Interview depth</h3><div class=\"qa\">\n    <div class=\"q\"><p class=\"qq\">What is eventual consistency? Give an example where it's fine and one where it's not. <span class=\"lvl\">Beginner</span></p><p>Replicas may briefly differ but converge if writes stop. Fine: Instagram like counts. Not fine: the last ticket for a concert, where two people must not both get it.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"q\"><p class=\"qq\">Why does a Raft cluster have an odd number of nodes? <span class=\"lvl\">Intermediate</span></p><p>It needs a majority to make progress. 3 nodes tolerate 1 failure, 4 nodes still tolerate only 1 (majority is 3), so the 4th adds cost without resilience. 5 tolerate 2.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"q\"><p class=\"qq\">How do you keep data consistent across microservices without 2PC? <span class=\"lvl\">Senior</span></p><p>Each service owns its data and commits locally. Publish events reliably with the transactional outbox (write event to an outbox table in the same transaction, a relay sends it to Kafka). Coordinate multi-step flows with a saga that has compensating actions. Make every consumer idempotent. Accept eventual consistency and design the UI around it (\"Order placed, confirming\u2026\").</p></div>\n    <div class=\"q\"><p class=\"qq\">How does Google Docs let two people type at once? <span class=\"lvl\">Senior</span></p><p>Operational Transformation (Google Docs) or CRDTs (Figma-like, Yjs): each edit is an operation that can be transformed or merged so every client ends with the same text regardless of the order edits arrive. A server orders operations and broadcasts them over WebSockets.</p></div>\n  </div></div>\n  <p class=\"say\"><b>Say this in the interview:</b> \"Seat inventory needs strong consistency, so it goes through one primary with row locks. The rest of the order flow is a saga with compensations, and the feed and notifications are eventually consistent.\"</p>\n</section>\n\n<!-- ============ 07 ============ -->\n<section class=\"topic\" id=\"t7\">\n  <header class=\"th\"><span class=\"num\">07</span><div>\n    <p class=\"eyebrow\">Do it later, do it reliably</p>\n    <h2>Message queues and event streaming</h2>\n    <p class=\"lede\">A queue lets one part of the system hand work to another without waiting for it. The user gets a fast response, slow work happens in the background, and spikes are absorbed instead of crashing things.</p>\n  </div></header>\n  <p class=\"analogy\"><b>Think of it like</b>a restaurant order slip rail. The waiter clips the order and goes back to customers. Cooks pick slips at their own speed. If 50 orders arrive at once, slips pile up on the rail, but nobody is turned away and nothing is forgotten.</p>\n  <figure class=\"dg\"><div class=\"scroll\">\n  <svg viewBox=\"0 0 780 280\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Producers write to a Kafka topic with three partitions; consumer groups read independently; failures go to a dead-letter queue\">\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"120\" height=\"48\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"70\" y=\"76\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Order service</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"70\" y=\"93\" text-anchor=\"middle\">producer</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"10\" y=\"140\" width=\"120\" height=\"48\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"70\" y=\"161\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Payment service</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"70\" y=\"178\" text-anchor=\"middle\">producer</text>\n    <path class=\"ea\" d=\"M130,79 L197,105\" marker-end=\"url(#ara)\"/><path class=\"ea\" d=\"M130,164 L197,140\" marker-end=\"url(#ara)\"/>\n    <rect class=\"na\" x=\"200\" y=\"30\" width=\"260\" height=\"190\" rx=\"8\"/>\n    <text class=\"t\" x=\"215\" y=\"52\">Kafka topic: order-events</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"215\" y=\"66\" width=\"230\" height=\"38\" rx=\"4\"/><text class=\"ts\" x=\"227\" y=\"90\">P0</text>\n    <rect class=\"dot\" x=\"255\" y=\"77\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" rx=\"2\"/><rect class=\"dot\" x=\"275\" y=\"77\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" rx=\"2\"/><rect class=\"dot\" x=\"295\" y=\"77\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" rx=\"2\"/><rect class=\"dot\" x=\"315\" y=\"77\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" rx=\"2\"/><text class=\"ts\" x=\"345\" y=\"90\">offset 0\u20133</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"215\" y=\"114\" width=\"230\" height=\"38\" rx=\"4\"/><text class=\"ts\" x=\"227\" y=\"138\">P1</text>\n    <rect class=\"dot\" x=\"255\" y=\"125\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" rx=\"2\"/><rect class=\"dot\" x=\"275\" y=\"125\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" rx=\"2\"/><text class=\"ts\" x=\"345\" y=\"138\">offset 0\u20131</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"215\" y=\"162\" width=\"230\" height=\"38\" rx=\"4\"/><text class=\"ts\" x=\"227\" y=\"186\">P2</text>\n    <rect class=\"dot\" x=\"255\" y=\"173\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" rx=\"2\"/><rect class=\"dot\" x=\"275\" y=\"173\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" rx=\"2\"/><rect class=\"dot\" x=\"295\" y=\"173\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" rx=\"2\"/><text class=\"ts\" x=\"345\" y=\"186\">offset 0\u20132</text>\n    <text class=\"ts\" x=\"330\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\">key = order_id \u2192 same order always same partition,</text>\n    <text class=\"ts\" x=\"330\" y=\"254\" text-anchor=\"middle\">so its events stay in order. Kept 7 days, replayable.</text>\n    <rect class=\"no\" x=\"540\" y=\"30\" width=\"150\" height=\"48\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"615\" y=\"51\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Email worker</text><text class=\"tg\" x=\"615\" y=\"68\" text-anchor=\"middle\">group: notify</text>\n    <rect class=\"no\" x=\"540\" y=\"100\" width=\"150\" height=\"48\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"615\" y=\"121\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Analytics</text><text class=\"tg\" x=\"615\" y=\"138\" text-anchor=\"middle\">group: analytics</text>\n    <rect class=\"nw\" x=\"540\" y=\"190\" width=\"150\" height=\"48\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"615\" y=\"211\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Dead-letter queue</text><text class=\"tb\" x=\"615\" y=\"228\" text-anchor=\"middle\">poison messages</text>\n    <path class=\"e\" d=\"M460,95 L537,57\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/><path class=\"e\" d=\"M460,125 L537,125\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/>\n    <path class=\"eb\" d=\"M690,60 C750,60 750,214 693,214\" marker-end=\"url(#arb)\"/>\n    <text class=\"tb\" x=\"715\" y=\"140\" text-anchor=\"middle\">after 3</text><text class=\"tb\" x=\"715\" y=\"154\" text-anchor=\"middle\">retries</text>\n  </svg></div>\n  <figcaption>Each consumer group gets every message and tracks its own position (offset). Within a group, partitions are split among workers, so 3 partitions allow up to 3 parallel workers.</figcaption></figure>\n\n  <div class=\"cols\">\n    <div><h3>Queue vs stream vs pub/sub</h3><ul>\n      <li><b>Message queue</b> (RabbitMQ, SQS): each message goes to one worker and is deleted when done. Task distribution: send email, resize image, generate invoice.</li>\n      <li><b>Event stream / log</b> (Kafka, Kinesis): messages are kept for days; many independent consumers read at their own pace and can replay. Event-driven systems, analytics, activity feeds.</li>\n      <li><b>Pub/sub</b> (Redis pub/sub, SNS, Google Pub/Sub): one message fans out to all subscribers. Notifications, cache invalidation, chat fan-out.</li>\n    </ul></div>\n    <div><h3>Delivery guarantees</h3><ul>\n      <li><b>At-most-once</b>: may lose messages, never duplicates. Metrics where a gap is OK.</li>\n      <li><b>At-least-once</b>: never lost, may duplicate. The usual default. Make consumers <b>idempotent</b>.</li>\n      <li><b>Exactly-once</b>: Kafka offers it within Kafka via transactions; end to end you achieve it as at-least-once + idempotent processing.</li>\n      <li><b>Ordering</b>: Kafka orders only within a partition, so choose the partition key carefully (order_id, user_id). SQS FIFO orders within a message group.</li>\n    </ul></div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div><h3>Tools & frameworks</h3><div class=\"tw\"><table>\n    <tr><th>Tool</th><th>Type</th><th>Pick it when</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Apache Kafka (Confluent, AWS MSK, Redpanda)</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Event stream</span></td><td>High throughput (millions/s), replay, many consumers, event sourcing. Used by LinkedIn, Uber, Swiggy</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>RabbitMQ</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Broker</span></td><td>Complex routing (topic/fanout exchanges), task queues, priorities, moderate scale</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>AWS SQS / SNS</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Managed queue / fan-out</span></td><td>Simplest reliable queue on AWS, no servers; SNS \u2192 many SQS queues for fan-out</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Google Pub/Sub, Azure Service Bus</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Managed</span></td><td>Same role on GCP/Azure</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>BullMQ (Node), Celery (Python), Sidekiq (Ruby)</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Job queues</span></td><td>Background jobs in your app backed by Redis: emails, retries, scheduled jobs</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Inngest, Trigger.dev, Supabase Queues</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Serverless jobs</span></td><td>Background jobs for Next.js/serverless apps without running workers yourself</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Apache Flink, Kafka Streams, Spark Streaming</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Stream processing</span></td><td>Real-time aggregations: fraud scores, ad-click counts, surge pricing</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Debezium</td><td><span class=\"tag\">CDC</span></td><td>Turn database changes into Kafka events (powers outbox and search sync)</td></tr>\n  </table></div></div>\n\n  <div><h3>What breaks, and how we prevent it</h3><div class=\"fails\">\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Worker crashes mid-task and the job is lost</p><p class=\"fix\">Acknowledge only after success; unacked messages return to the queue after a visibility timeout.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Duplicate processing sends 2 emails or charges twice</p><p class=\"fix\">Idempotency: dedupe table of processed message IDs, unique constraints, upserts.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">A bad message fails forever and blocks the queue</p><p class=\"fix\">Retry with exponential backoff, then move to a dead-letter queue and alert.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">DB commit succeeds but publishing the event fails</p><p class=\"fix\">Transactional outbox: write the event to an outbox table in the same DB transaction; a relay (Debezium) publishes it.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Consumers fall behind (consumer lag grows)</p><p class=\"fix\">Monitor lag, add partitions and consumers, batch processing, autoscale workers on queue depth.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Producers flood the system during a spike</p><p class=\"fix\">Back-pressure: bounded queues, rate limits at the producer, queue absorbs the burst while workers drain at a steady rate.</p></div>\n  </div></div>\n\n  <div><h3>Interview depth</h3><div class=\"qa\">\n    <div class=\"q\"><p class=\"qq\">Why add a queue between upload and video processing? <span class=\"lvl\">Beginner</span></p><p>Encoding takes minutes. The upload API returns immediately (\"processing\"), puts a job in the queue, and a pool of workers encodes at their own pace. If 10\u00d7 uploads arrive, the queue grows instead of servers falling over, and failed jobs are retried.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"q\"><p class=\"qq\">Kafka or RabbitMQ? <span class=\"lvl\">Intermediate</span></p><p>Kafka when you need very high throughput, ordering per key, retention and replay, or several teams consuming the same events. RabbitMQ when you need flexible routing, per-message acknowledgement, priorities and simpler operations for task queues. SQS when you just want a managed queue on AWS.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"q\"><p class=\"qq\">How would you send a notification to 10 M followers when a celebrity posts? <span class=\"lvl\">Senior</span></p><p>The post service publishes one event. A fan-out service reads follower IDs in batches and enqueues delivery jobs partitioned by user. Workers call APNs/FCM with rate limits per provider, with retries and a DLQ. For feeds, celebrities use fan-out-on-read (merge their posts at read time) instead of writing to 10 M inboxes.</p></div>\n  </div></div>\n  <p class=\"say\"><b>Say this in the interview:</b> \"Placing the order is synchronous. Everything after it (email, invoice, analytics, rider assignment) is asynchronous through Kafka, keyed by order_id, with idempotent consumers and a dead-letter queue.\"</p>\n</section>\n\n<!-- ============ 08 ============ -->\n<section class=\"topic\" id=\"t8\">\n  <header class=\"th\"><span class=\"num\">08</span><div>\n    <p class=\"eyebrow\">The shape of the system</p>\n    <h2>Architecture patterns</h2>\n    <p class=\"lede\">How you split the system into pieces: one big application, many small services, functions that run on demand, or services that talk through events. Each shape suits a different team size and scale.</p>\n  </div></header>\n  <p class=\"analogy\"><b>Think of it like</b>a kitchen. A monolith is one chef cooking everything: simple, until the restaurant gets huge. Microservices are stations (tandoor, chinese, desserts) each with its own cook and pantry, working independently and passing tickets. More throughput, but now you need coordination.</p>\n  <figure class=\"dg\"><div class=\"scroll\">\n  <svg viewBox=\"0 0 780 300\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Left: monolith with modules sharing one database. Right: client through API gateway to separate services each with its own database and a shared event bus\">\n    <text class=\"tt\" x=\"120\" y=\"20\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Monolith</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"20\" y=\"35\" width=\"200\" height=\"200\" rx=\"8\"/>\n    <rect class=\"na\" x=\"35\" y=\"50\" width=\"170\" height=\"30\" rx=\"4\"/><text class=\"ts\" x=\"120\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Users module</text>\n    <rect class=\"na\" x=\"35\" y=\"88\" width=\"170\" height=\"30\" rx=\"4\"/><text class=\"ts\" x=\"120\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Orders module</text>\n    <rect class=\"na\" x=\"35\" y=\"126\" width=\"170\" height=\"30\" rx=\"4\"/><text class=\"ts\" x=\"120\" y=\"146\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Payments module</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"60\" y=\"178\" width=\"120\" height=\"42\" rx=\"20\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"120\" y=\"204\" text-anchor=\"middle\">One database</text>\n    <text class=\"ts\" x=\"120\" y=\"258\" text-anchor=\"middle\">One deploy, one codebase</text>\n    <text class=\"tg\" x=\"120\" y=\"274\" text-anchor=\"middle\">fast to build, easy to debug</text>\n    <line class=\"sep\" x1=\"250\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"250\" y2=\"290\"/>\n    <text class=\"tt\" x=\"515\" y=\"20\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Microservices</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"265\" y=\"115\" width=\"70\" height=\"44\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"300\" y=\"141\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Client</text>\n    <rect class=\"na\" x=\"360\" y=\"105\" width=\"110\" height=\"64\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"415\" y=\"132\" text-anchor=\"middle\">API gateway</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"415\" y=\"151\" text-anchor=\"middle\">auth \u00b7 route</text>\n    <path class=\"e\" d=\"M335,137 L357,137\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"510\" y=\"35\" width=\"120\" height=\"44\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"570\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\">User service</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"510\" y=\"115\" width=\"120\" height=\"44\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"570\" y=\"142\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Order service</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"510\" y=\"195\" width=\"120\" height=\"44\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"570\" y=\"222\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Payment service</text>\n    <path class=\"e\" d=\"M470,125 L507,60\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/><path class=\"e\" d=\"M470,137 L507,137\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/><path class=\"e\" d=\"M470,149 L507,214\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"660\" y=\"40\" width=\"100\" height=\"34\" rx=\"16\"/><text class=\"ts\" x=\"710\" y=\"61\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Postgres</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"660\" y=\"120\" width=\"100\" height=\"34\" rx=\"16\"/><text class=\"ts\" x=\"710\" y=\"141\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Postgres</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"660\" y=\"200\" width=\"100\" height=\"34\" rx=\"16\"/><text class=\"ts\" x=\"710\" y=\"221\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Postgres</text>\n    <path class=\"e\" d=\"M630,57 L657,57\"/><path class=\"e\" d=\"M630,137 L657,137\"/><path class=\"e\" d=\"M630,217 L657,217\"/>\n    <rect class=\"no\" x=\"360\" y=\"262\" width=\"400\" height=\"30\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"560\" y=\"282\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Event bus (Kafka): OrderPlaced, PaymentCaptured\u2026</text>\n    <path class=\"nd\" d=\"M570,239 L570,262\"/><path class=\"nd\" d=\"M545,159 C500,190 500,240 520,262\"/>\n    <text class=\"ts\" x=\"415\" y=\"200\" text-anchor=\"middle\">each service owns</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"415\" y=\"214\" text-anchor=\"middle\">its data, deploys alone</text>\n  </svg></div>\n  <figcaption>Interview tip: start with a well-structured monolith (or modular monolith) for a new product; split into services only where team size, scale or deploy speed demands it.</figcaption></figure>\n\n  <div><h3>Patterns: which one, when</h3><div class=\"tw\"><table>\n    <tr><th>Pattern</th><th>What it is</th><th>Use when</th><th>Cost</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Monolith</td><td>One deployable app, one DB</td><td>Startups, small teams (&lt;10 devs), MVPs</td><td>Hard to scale teams; one bug can take down everything</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Modular monolith</td><td>One deploy, strict internal module boundaries</td><td>Growing products that may split later (Shopify runs this way)</td><td>Needs discipline to keep modules separate</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Microservices</td><td>Small services, own DB, own deploy</td><td>Many teams, parts with very different scaling needs</td><td>Network failures, distributed data, ops overhead</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Serverless</td><td>Functions run per request; you don't manage servers</td><td>Spiky or low traffic, webhooks, cron jobs, image resizing</td><td>Cold starts, time limits, vendor lock-in, cost at high steady load</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Event-driven</td><td>Services react to published events</td><td>Many things must happen after one action</td><td>Harder to trace flows; eventual consistency</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>CQRS</td><td>Separate models for writes (commands) and reads (queries)</td><td>Reads and writes have very different shapes or scale (dashboards, feeds)</td><td>Two models to keep in sync</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Event sourcing</td><td>Store every change as an event; current state = replay</td><td>Audit trails: banking ledgers, order history</td><td>Complex; needs snapshots; schema evolution is hard</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Backend-for-frontend (BFF)</td><td>A thin API per client type (web, mobile)</td><td>Mobile and web need different data shapes</td><td>More services to maintain</td></tr>\n  </table></div></div>\n\n  <div><h3>Tools & frameworks</h3><div class=\"tw\"><table>\n    <tr><th>Tool</th><th>Category</th><th>Role</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Docker</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Containers</span></td><td>Package each service with its dependencies so it runs the same everywhere</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Kubernetes (EKS, GKE, AKS), ECS, Nomad</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Orchestration</span></td><td>Run, scale, heal and roll out containers</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Istio, Linkerd</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Service mesh</span></td><td>mTLS between services, retries, traffic splitting, telemetry without code changes</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Consul, Eureka, Kubernetes DNS</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Service discovery</span></td><td>Services find each other's current addresses</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>AWS Lambda, Cloudflare Workers, Vercel Functions, Supabase Edge Functions</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Serverless</span></td><td>Run code on demand at the edge or in region</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Spring Boot, NestJS, Go kit, FastAPI</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Service frameworks</span></td><td>Building individual services</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Terraform, Pulumi, AWS CDK</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Infra as code</span></td><td>Recreate all infrastructure from code, reviewed like code</td></tr>\n  </table></div></div>\n\n  <div><h3>What breaks, and how we prevent it</h3><div class=\"fails\">\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Distributed monolith: services must deploy together</p><p class=\"fix\">Split by business capability (bounded contexts), own data per service, version APIs, communicate via events.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">One slow service makes every caller slow (cascading failure)</p><p class=\"fix\">Timeouts, circuit breakers, bulkheads and async calls (topic 9).</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Can't find where a request failed across 12 services</p><p class=\"fix\">Distributed tracing with a trace ID passed through every call (topic 12).</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Shared database couples services</p><p class=\"fix\">Database per service; others get data through APIs or events, never by reading your tables.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Serverless cold starts slow the first request</p><p class=\"fix\">Provisioned concurrency, smaller bundles, edge runtimes, keep latency-critical paths on long-running servers.</p></div>\n  </div></div>\n\n  <div><h3>Interview depth</h3><div class=\"qa\">\n    <div class=\"q\"><p class=\"qq\">Monolith or microservices for a new startup? <span class=\"lvl\">Beginner</span></p><p>Monolith (ideally modular). It's faster to build, test and deploy with a small team. Microservices solve organizational and scaling problems the startup doesn't have yet, while adding network and data complexity.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"q\"><p class=\"qq\">How do you split a monolith? <span class=\"lvl\">Intermediate</span></p><p>Strangler fig pattern: put a gateway in front, carve out one well-bounded domain at a time (e.g. notifications, then payments), route its traffic to the new service, migrate its data, and retire the old code. Start with parts that change often or scale differently.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"q\"><p class=\"qq\">When would you use CQRS? <span class=\"lvl\">Senior</span></p><p>When reads need a very different shape than writes, for example an order system writes normalized rows in Postgres but the seller dashboard reads pre-aggregated views in Elasticsearch or a materialized table. Events from the write side update the read side, which can lag by seconds.</p></div>\n  </div></div>\n  <p class=\"say\"><b>Say this in the interview:</b> \"I'll start with services for the parts that scale differently, like the location service and the order service, behind an API gateway, communicating through Kafka events, each owning its database.\"</p>\n</section>\n\n<!-- ============ 09 ============ -->\n<section class=\"topic\" id=\"t9\">\n  <header class=\"th\"><span class=\"num\">09</span><div>\n    <p class=\"eyebrow\">Failing gracefully</p>\n    <h2>Resilience: rate limiting, circuit breakers, retries, disaster recovery</h2>\n    <p class=\"lede\">Everything fails eventually: servers, networks, third-party APIs, whole regions. Resilience patterns make sure one failure stays small instead of spreading through the whole system.</p>\n  </div></header>\n  <p class=\"analogy\"><b>Think of it like</b>the electrical fuse box in your house. A rate limiter is the meter that caps how much power one appliance can draw. A circuit breaker trips when something shorts, so the fault doesn't burn down the whole house, and you try switching it back on after a while.</p>\n  <figure class=\"dg\"><div class=\"scroll\">\n  <svg viewBox=\"0 0 780 280\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Left: token bucket rate limiter. Right: circuit breaker states closed, open, half-open\">\n    <text class=\"tt\" x=\"170\" y=\"20\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Token bucket rate limiter</text>\n    <text class=\"ts\" x=\"100\" y=\"48\" text-anchor=\"middle\">refill +10 tokens/sec</text>\n    <path class=\"ea\" d=\"M100,55 L100,80\" marker-end=\"url(#ara)\"/>\n    <path class=\"na\" d=\"M40,85 L40,200 Q40,212 52,212 L148,212 Q160,212 160,200 L160,85\"/>\n    <circle class=\"dot\" cx=\"65\" cy=\"195\" r=\"8\"/><circle class=\"dot\" cx=\"88\" cy=\"195\" r=\"8\"/><circle class=\"dot\" cx=\"111\" cy=\"195\" r=\"8\"/><circle class=\"dot\" cx=\"134\" cy=\"195\" r=\"8\"/>\n    <circle class=\"dot\" cx=\"76\" cy=\"175\" r=\"8\"/><circle class=\"dot\" cx=\"99\" cy=\"175\" r=\"8\"/><circle class=\"dot\" cx=\"122\" cy=\"175\" r=\"8\"/>\n    <text class=\"ts\" x=\"100\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\">capacity 20</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"100\" y=\"142\" text-anchor=\"middle\">(allows bursts)</text>\n    <path class=\"eg\" d=\"M165,150 L215,150\" marker-end=\"url(#arg)\"/>\n    <text class=\"tg\" x=\"222\" y=\"140\">token left \u2192</text><text class=\"tg\" x=\"222\" y=\"155\">request allowed</text>\n    <text class=\"tb\" x=\"222\" y=\"190\">bucket empty \u2192</text><text class=\"tb\" x=\"222\" y=\"205\">429 Too Many Requests</text>\n    <text class=\"ts\" x=\"170\" y=\"250\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Key per user, IP or API key, stored in Redis</text>\n    <text class=\"ts\" x=\"170\" y=\"264\" text-anchor=\"middle\">so every server shares the same counts</text>\n    <line class=\"sep\" x1=\"360\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"360\" y2=\"272\"/>\n    <text class=\"tt\" x=\"570\" y=\"20\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Circuit breaker</text>\n    <rect class=\"no\" x=\"385\" y=\"70\" width=\"130\" height=\"54\" rx=\"27\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"450\" y=\"93\" text-anchor=\"middle\">CLOSED</text><text class=\"tg\" x=\"450\" y=\"110\" text-anchor=\"middle\">calls pass</text>\n    <rect class=\"nw\" x=\"630\" y=\"70\" width=\"130\" height=\"54\" rx=\"27\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"695\" y=\"93\" text-anchor=\"middle\">OPEN</text><text class=\"tb\" x=\"695\" y=\"110\" text-anchor=\"middle\">fail fast</text>\n    <rect class=\"ny\" x=\"505\" y=\"190\" width=\"130\" height=\"54\" rx=\"27\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"570\" y=\"213\" text-anchor=\"middle\">HALF-OPEN</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"570\" y=\"230\" text-anchor=\"middle\">try 1 call</text>\n    <path class=\"eb\" d=\"M515,88 L627,88\" marker-end=\"url(#arb)\"/><text class=\"tb\" x=\"571\" y=\"78\" text-anchor=\"middle\">50% errors in 10 s</text>\n    <path class=\"e\" d=\"M695,124 L625,192\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/><text class=\"ts\" x=\"700\" y=\"165\">after 30 s</text>\n    <path class=\"eg\" d=\"M520,195 L470,127\" marker-end=\"url(#arg)\"/><text class=\"tg\" x=\"420\" y=\"168\">success</text>\n    <path class=\"eb\" d=\"M630,205 C700,205 735,170 718,127\" marker-end=\"url(#arb)\"/><text class=\"tb\" x=\"690\" y=\"232\">fails again</text>\n    <text class=\"ts\" x=\"570\" y=\"270\" text-anchor=\"middle\">While OPEN, return a fallback (cached data, \"try later\") instantly</text>\n  </svg></div>\n  <figcaption>Rate limiters protect you from your callers. Circuit breakers protect you from your dependencies.</figcaption></figure>\n\n  <div class=\"cols\">\n    <div><h3>Rate limiting algorithms</h3><ul>\n      <li><b>Token bucket</b>: tokens refill at a steady rate; each request spends one. Allows short bursts. Most common (AWS, Stripe).</li>\n      <li><b>Leaky bucket</b>: requests drain at a fixed rate, extras wait or drop. Smooth output, used for traffic shaping.</li>\n      <li><b>Fixed window</b>: 100 requests per minute, counter resets on the minute. Simple, but 200 can slip through at the boundary.</li>\n      <li><b>Sliding window log / counter</b>: counts over the last 60 s exactly or approximately. Fairer, slightly more memory.</li>\n      <li>Return <code>429</code> with a <code>Retry-After</code> header so clients know when to try again.</li>\n    </ul></div>\n    <div><h3>The resilience toolkit</h3><ul>\n      <li><b>Timeouts</b> on every network call. Without them, threads pile up waiting forever.</li>\n      <li><b>Retries with exponential backoff + jitter</b>: wait 100 ms, 200 ms, 400 ms\u2026 plus randomness, so clients don't retry in sync. Only retry idempotent operations.</li>\n      <li><b>Bulkheads</b>: separate thread or connection pools per dependency, so a slow payment API can't use up the pool needed for search.</li>\n      <li><b>Graceful degradation</b>: if recommendations fail, show \"popular items\" instead of an error page.</li>\n      <li><b>Load shedding</b>: when overloaded, drop low-priority traffic first (analytics before checkout).</li>\n      <li><b>Disaster recovery</b>: RPO = how much data you can lose; RTO = how long you can be down. Options from cheapest to fastest: backup & restore \u2192 pilot light \u2192 warm standby \u2192 active-active.</li>\n    </ul></div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div><h3>Tools & frameworks</h3><div class=\"tw\"><table>\n    <tr><th>Tool</th><th>Category</th><th>Use it for</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Redis + Lua scripts, Upstash Ratelimit</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Rate limiting</span></td><td>Distributed counters shared by all servers</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Kong, Nginx limit_req, Envoy, AWS API Gateway, Cloudflare</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Edge limiting</span></td><td>Limit before traffic reaches your code</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Resilience4j (Java), Polly (.NET), opossum (Node), Hystrix (legacy)</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Circuit breakers</span></td><td>Breakers, retries, bulkheads, timeouts in code</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Istio, Linkerd</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Mesh-level</span></td><td>Retries, timeouts, outlier ejection without changing code</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Cloudflare, AWS Shield, AWS WAF</td><td><span class=\"tag\">DDoS</span></td><td>Absorb and filter attack traffic</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Chaos Monkey, Gremlin, AWS FIS, LitmusChaos</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Chaos testing</span></td><td>Kill things on purpose to prove the system survives</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>AWS Backup, pgBackRest, Velero</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Backups / DR</span></td><td>Point-in-time recovery, cluster backups</td></tr>\n  </table></div></div>\n\n  <div><h3>What breaks, and how we prevent it</h3><div class=\"fails\">\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Retry storm: service recovers, every client retries at once, it dies again</p><p class=\"fix\">Exponential backoff with jitter, retry budgets (max 10% extra traffic), circuit breakers.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">A third-party API (SMS, payment) goes down</p><p class=\"fix\">Circuit breaker, fallback provider (Twilio \u2192 MSG91), queue the work and retry later.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Bots scrape or brute-force login</p><p class=\"fix\">Per-IP and per-account rate limits, CAPTCHA after failures, WAF rules.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Whole region goes down</p><p class=\"fix\">Multi-region with replicated data and DNS failover; regular game-day drills.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Backups exist but restore was never tested</p><p class=\"fix\">Automated restore tests on a schedule; measure actual RTO.</p></div>\n  </div></div>\n\n  <div><h3>Interview depth</h3><div class=\"qa\">\n    <div class=\"q\"><p class=\"qq\">Why do we need rate limiting? <span class=\"lvl\">Beginner</span></p><p>To protect the system from overload (spikes, bugs in clients, bots), to prevent abuse (brute force, scraping), to keep usage fair between customers, and to control cost on paid downstream APIs.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"q\"><p class=\"qq\">Design a distributed rate limiter. <span class=\"lvl\">Intermediate</span></p><p>Run it at the API gateway. For each request, build a key like <code>rl:{user_id}:{minute}</code>, run an atomic Redis Lua script that refills/decrements a token bucket and returns allow or deny. Redis Cluster shards keys. If Redis is down, fail open for most endpoints (allow) and fail closed for sensitive ones like login. Return headers: <code>X-RateLimit-Remaining</code>, <code>Retry-After</code>.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"q\"><p class=\"qq\">How do retries make outages worse, and how do you stop it? <span class=\"lvl\">Senior</span></p><p>With 3 retries at each of 4 layers, one user request can become 3\u2074 = 81 calls on the deepest service. Retry at only one layer, use backoff with jitter, cap retries with a budget, honour Retry-After, and let circuit breakers stop calls to a failing service entirely.</p></div>\n  </div></div>\n  <p class=\"say\"><b>Say this in the interview:</b> \"Every external call gets a timeout, retries with jittered backoff, and a circuit breaker with a fallback. The gateway rate-limits per user with a Redis token bucket.\"</p>\n</section>\n\n<!-- ============ 10 ============ -->\n<section class=\"topic\" id=\"t10\">\n  <header class=\"th\"><span class=\"num\">10</span><div>\n    <p class=\"eyebrow\">Files, search and special structures</p>\n    <h2>Object storage, search and clever data structures</h2>\n    <p class=\"lede\">Images, videos and documents don't belong in a database, and \"find products like 'red shose'\" can't be answered with SQL <code>LIKE</code>. This topic covers where big files go, how search engines work, and a few data structures interviewers love.</p>\n  </div></header>\n  <p class=\"analogy\"><b>Think of it like</b>a godown and an index card box. Big items (files) go to a cheap, huge godown (object storage), and the database only keeps the slip saying where each item is. A search index is the back-of-book index: for every word, the list of pages it appears on.</p>\n  <figure class=\"dg\"><div class=\"scroll\">\n  <svg viewBox=\"0 0 780 290\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Top: client uploads directly to object storage using a presigned URL, served via CDN. Bottom: database changes stream into a search index\">\n    <text class=\"tt\" x=\"10\" y=\"20\">Files &amp; images</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"10\" y=\"40\" width=\"110\" height=\"50\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"65\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Client</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"65\" y=\"79\" text-anchor=\"middle\">picks photo</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"200\" y=\"40\" width=\"130\" height=\"50\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"265\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\">App server</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"265\" y=\"79\" text-anchor=\"middle\">signs URL</text>\n    <rect class=\"na\" x=\"420\" y=\"40\" width=\"150\" height=\"50\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"495\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Object storage</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"495\" y=\"79\" text-anchor=\"middle\">S3 \u00b7 R2 \u00b7 GCS</text>\n    <rect class=\"na\" x=\"640\" y=\"40\" width=\"125\" height=\"50\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"702\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\">CDN</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"702\" y=\"79\" text-anchor=\"middle\">serves to users</text>\n    <path class=\"e\" d=\"M120,58 L197,58\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/><text class=\"ts\" x=\"158\" y=\"50\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\u2460 ask</text>\n    <path class=\"e\" d=\"M197,74 L122,74\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/><text class=\"ts\" x=\"158\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\u2461 URL</text>\n    <path class=\"ea\" d=\"M65,90 C65,135 495,135 495,93\" marker-end=\"url(#ara)\"/><text class=\"ts\" x=\"280\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\u2462 upload file directly (app server never carries the bytes)</text>\n    <path class=\"e\" d=\"M570,65 L637,65\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/><text class=\"ts\" x=\"603\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\u2463</text>\n    <line class=\"sep\" x1=\"10\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"770\" y2=\"150\"/>\n    <text class=\"tt\" x=\"10\" y=\"175\">Search</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"10\" y=\"200\" width=\"130\" height=\"54\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"75\" y=\"223\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Primary DB</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"75\" y=\"241\" text-anchor=\"middle\">source of truth</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"200\" y=\"200\" width=\"150\" height=\"54\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"275\" y=\"223\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Change stream</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"275\" y=\"241\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Debezium \u2192 Kafka</text>\n    <rect class=\"na\" x=\"410\" y=\"200\" width=\"160\" height=\"54\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"490\" y=\"223\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Elasticsearch</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"490\" y=\"241\" text-anchor=\"middle\">inverted index</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"630\" y=\"200\" width=\"135\" height=\"54\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"697\" y=\"223\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Search API</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"697\" y=\"241\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\"red shoes\"</text>\n    <path class=\"e\" d=\"M140,227 L197,227\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/><path class=\"e\" d=\"M350,227 L407,227\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/><path class=\"e\" d=\"M627,227 L573,227\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/>\n    <text class=\"ts\" x=\"390\" y=\"280\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Index is a copy, updated within seconds. If it breaks, rebuild it from the DB.</text>\n  </svg></div>\n  <figcaption>Two patterns worth drawing in almost any design: presigned uploads for media, and change-data-capture to keep a search index in sync.</figcaption></figure>\n\n  <div class=\"cols\">\n    <div><h3>Storage types</h3><ul>\n      <li><b>Object storage</b> (S3, Cloudflare R2, GCS): files addressed by key, virtually unlimited, 11 nines durability, cheap. Images, videos, backups, exports.</li>\n      <li><b>Block storage</b> (EBS): a virtual hard disk attached to one server. Databases run on it.</li>\n      <li><b>File storage</b> (EFS, NFS): shared folders mounted by many servers.</li>\n      <li><b>Storage tiers</b>: S3 Standard \u2192 Infrequent Access \u2192 Glacier for old data. Lifecycle rules move data automatically.</li>\n      <li><b>Large uploads</b>: multipart upload in chunks (5\u2013100 MB) with resume; Dropbox also dedupes chunks by hash.</li>\n      <li><b>Data lake / warehouse</b>: raw files in S3 (lake) or structured analytics tables (Snowflake, BigQuery, Redshift, ClickHouse) for reporting, never on your production DB.</li>\n    </ul></div>\n    <div><h3>How search works</h3><ul>\n      <li><b>Inverted index</b>: \"red\" \u2192 [doc 4, 9, 22], \"shoes\" \u2192 [doc 9, 22, 31]. Intersect lists to find documents with both words, instantly.</li>\n      <li><b>Analyzers</b>: lowercasing, stemming (\"running\" \u2192 \"run\"), synonyms, n-grams for autocomplete.</li>\n      <li><b>Fuzzy matching</b>: edit distance handles typos like \"shose\".</li>\n      <li><b>Ranking</b>: BM25 relevance score plus business signals (popularity, rating, stock).</li>\n      <li><b>Autocomplete</b>: a trie or prefix index of popular queries, cached heavily; results computed offline from search logs.</li>\n      <li><b>Semantic search</b>: embeddings in a vector index (pgvector, Pinecone) find meaning, not just words; often combined with keyword search (hybrid).</li>\n    </ul></div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div><h3>Data structures interviewers love</h3><div class=\"tw\"><table>\n    <tr><th>Structure</th><th>What it answers</th><th>Where it's used</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Bloom filter</td><td>\"Is this item definitely NOT in the set?\" with tiny memory; may give false positives</td><td>Skip DB lookups for unknown keys, Chrome malicious URL check, Cassandra, \"username taken?\"</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>HyperLogLog</td><td>\"Roughly how many unique items?\" using ~12 KB for billions</td><td>Unique visitors, unique viewers on YouTube, Redis PFCOUNT</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Count-Min Sketch</td><td>\"About how often did X occur?\"</td><td>Trending hashtags, top-K queries, heavy hitters</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Trie</td><td>All words with a given prefix</td><td>Autocomplete, typeahead</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Geohash / Quadtree / H3</td><td>\"What's near this location?\"</td><td>Uber driver matching, Swiggy restaurants near you, Zomato delivery radius</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Sorted set (skip list)</td><td>Ranked items with fast insert and range</td><td>Leaderboards (Redis ZSET), rate-limit logs</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Merkle tree</td><td>Which parts of two big datasets differ</td><td>Replica repair in Cassandra/DynamoDB, Git, blockchains</td></tr>\n  </table></div></div>\n\n  <div><h3>Tools & frameworks</h3><div class=\"tw\"><table>\n    <tr><th>Tool</th><th>Category</th><th>Pick it when</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>AWS S3, Cloudflare R2, Google Cloud Storage, Supabase Storage, MinIO</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Object storage</span></td><td>Any user files; R2 has no egress fees; MinIO for self-hosted (e.g. a team file server)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Elasticsearch, OpenSearch</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Search</span></td><td>Large-scale full-text search, log search, faceted filters</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Algolia, Typesense, Meilisearch</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Hosted / simple search</span></td><td>Fast instant search for e-commerce without running a cluster</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Postgres full-text, pg_trgm, pgvector</td><td><span class=\"tag\">In-DB search</span></td><td>Small to medium datasets; avoid a new system</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>FFmpeg, AWS MediaConvert, Mux, Cloudinary, imgproxy</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Media processing</span></td><td>Transcoding video to HLS/DASH, resizing images</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Snowflake, BigQuery, ClickHouse, Redshift</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Analytics</span></td><td>Reporting and dashboards on huge data</td></tr>\n  </table></div></div>\n\n  <div><h3>What breaks, and how we prevent it</h3><div class=\"fails\">\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Large uploads choke app servers</p><p class=\"fix\">Presigned URLs so clients upload straight to S3; multipart and resumable uploads.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Search index out of sync with DB</p><p class=\"fix\">CDC-based sync instead of dual writes, periodic full reindex, alias swap for zero-downtime rebuilds.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Huge bandwidth bill for popular images</p><p class=\"fix\">CDN in front, image resizing and WebP/AVIF, long cache headers.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Public bucket leaks private files</p><p class=\"fix\">Private buckets by default, short-lived signed URLs, bucket policies audited.</p></div>\n  </div></div>\n\n  <div><h3>Interview depth</h3><div class=\"qa\">\n    <div class=\"q\"><p class=\"qq\">Why not store images in the database? <span class=\"lvl\">Beginner</span></p><p>It bloats the DB, makes backups huge and slow, wastes expensive DB I/O, and can't be served by a CDN easily. Store the file in object storage and keep only its URL and metadata in the DB.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"q\"><p class=\"qq\">How would you find drivers within 2 km? <span class=\"lvl\">Intermediate</span></p><p>Encode locations as geohashes (or H3 cells). Drivers update their cell in Redis (GEOADD) every few seconds. To search, look up the user's cell and its 8 neighbours, then compute exact distance and filter. Shard by city or region.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"q\"><p class=\"qq\">Design typeahead for 10 k QPS. <span class=\"lvl\">Senior</span></p><p>Offline job aggregates search logs into top queries per prefix and builds a trie (or prefix \u2192 top-10 map). Serve it from memory or Redis, replicated per region, with the CDN caching popular prefixes. Client debounces typing (~150 ms). Trending terms are merged in from a streaming job.</p></div>\n  </div></div>\n  <p class=\"say\"><b>Say this in the interview:</b> \"Media goes to S3 via presigned URLs and is served through a CDN. Product search runs on Elasticsearch fed by CDC from Postgres, so the DB stays the source of truth.\"</p>\n</section>\n\n<!-- ============ 11 ============ -->\n<section class=\"topic\" id=\"t11\">\n  <header class=\"th\"><span class=\"num\">11</span><div>\n    <p class=\"eyebrow\">Who are you, what may you do</p>\n    <h2>Security: authentication, authorization, encryption</h2>\n    <p class=\"lede\">Authentication proves who you are. Authorization decides what you're allowed to do. Encryption keeps data unreadable to anyone in between. Mentioning these briefly in every design shows maturity.</p>\n  </div></header>\n  <p class=\"analogy\"><b>Think of it like</b>an office building. At reception you show ID and get a visitor badge (authentication \u2192 token). The badge only opens floors you're allowed on (authorization). The badge expires at 6 pm (token expiry). Confidential papers travel in locked bags (encryption).</p>\n  <figure class=\"dg\"><div class=\"scroll\">\n  <svg viewBox=\"0 0 780 270\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"User logs in to auth server, receives JWT, sends it to API gateway which verifies before calling service\">\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"10\" y=\"105\" width=\"110\" height=\"54\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"65\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\">User / app</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"65\" y=\"146\" text-anchor=\"middle\">stores token</text>\n    <rect class=\"na\" x=\"220\" y=\"20\" width=\"170\" height=\"58\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"305\" y=\"44\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Auth server</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"305\" y=\"63\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Auth0 \u00b7 Cognito \u00b7 Supabase</text>\n    <rect class=\"na\" x=\"220\" y=\"190\" width=\"170\" height=\"58\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"305\" y=\"214\" text-anchor=\"middle\">API gateway</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"305\" y=\"233\" text-anchor=\"middle\">verifies signature</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"480\" y=\"190\" width=\"150\" height=\"58\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"555\" y=\"214\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Order service</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"555\" y=\"233\" text-anchor=\"middle\">checks role/owner</text>\n    <path class=\"ea\" d=\"M100,105 L217,48\" marker-end=\"url(#ara)\"/><text class=\"ts\" x=\"118\" y=\"66\">\u2460 log in</text>\n    <path class=\"eg\" d=\"M217,66 L120,120\" marker-end=\"url(#arg)\"/><text class=\"tg\" x=\"182\" y=\"112\">\u2461 tokens</text>\n    <path class=\"ea\" d=\"M100,159 L217,212\" marker-end=\"url(#ara)\"/><text class=\"ts\" x=\"88\" y=\"205\">\u2462 Bearer token</text>\n    <path class=\"e\" d=\"M390,219 L477,219\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/><text class=\"ts\" x=\"433\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\u2463 allowed</text>\n    <rect class=\"nd\" x=\"460\" y=\"18\" width=\"305\" height=\"132\" rx=\"6\"/>\n    <text class=\"t\" x=\"475\" y=\"40\">JWT = header . payload . signature</text>\n    <text class=\"ts\" x=\"475\" y=\"62\">{ \"sub\": \"user_42\",</text>\n    <text class=\"ts\" x=\"475\" y=\"78\">  \"role\": \"customer\",</text>\n    <text class=\"ts\" x=\"475\" y=\"94\">  \"exp\": now + 15 min }</text>\n    <text class=\"tg\" x=\"475\" y=\"118\">Signed by auth server's private key;</text>\n    <text class=\"tg\" x=\"475\" y=\"134\">gateway checks with public key, no DB call</text>\n    <path class=\"nd\" d=\"M390,49 L457,60\"/>\n  </svg></div>\n  <figcaption>Short-lived access token (15 min) + long-lived refresh token (stored in an httpOnly cookie) is the standard pattern.</figcaption></figure>\n\n  <div class=\"cols\">\n    <div><h3>Authentication options</h3><ul>\n      <li><b>Session cookie</b>: server stores session in Redis, browser holds a session ID. Easy to revoke. Great for web apps.</li>\n      <li><b>JWT</b>: self-contained signed token, no lookup needed. Scales across services; hard to revoke early, so keep it short-lived.</li>\n      <li><b>OAuth 2.0</b>: lets an app act on your behalf without your password (\"Allow access to Google Drive\").</li>\n      <li><b>OpenID Connect (OIDC)</b>: login built on OAuth (\"Sign in with Google\").</li>\n      <li><b>SSO / SAML</b>: one corporate login for all work apps (Okta).</li>\n      <li><b>API keys</b> for server-to-server; <b>mTLS</b> for service-to-service inside a mesh.</li>\n      <li><b>MFA / OTP / passkeys</b> for sensitive actions.</li>\n    </ul></div>\n    <div><h3>Authorization & data protection</h3><ul>\n      <li><b>RBAC</b>: roles (admin, seller, customer) grant permissions.</li>\n      <li><b>ABAC / ownership</b>: \"a user may edit only orders where order.user_id = their id\". Always check on the server.</li>\n      <li><b>Row Level Security</b>: Postgres/Supabase enforce ownership rules inside the DB.</li>\n      <li><b>Encryption in transit</b>: TLS everywhere, including internal traffic.</li>\n      <li><b>Encryption at rest</b>: disk/DB encryption with keys in KMS.</li>\n      <li><b>Passwords</b>: never store them; store bcrypt/argon2 hashes with salt.</li>\n      <li><b>Secrets</b>: in Vault or a secrets manager, never in code or Git.</li>\n      <li><b>PCI</b>: never store raw card numbers; use Razorpay/Stripe tokenization.</li>\n    </ul></div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div><h3>Tools & frameworks</h3><div class=\"tw\"><table>\n    <tr><th>Tool</th><th>Category</th><th>Use it for</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Auth0, AWS Cognito, Firebase Auth, Supabase Auth, Clerk, Keycloak</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Identity provider</span></td><td>Sign-up, login, social login, MFA without building it yourself</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>NextAuth / Auth.js, Passport.js, Spring Security</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Framework auth</span></td><td>Auth inside your app framework</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Okta, Azure AD (Entra ID)</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Enterprise SSO</span></td><td>Company-wide single sign-on, SAML</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>HashiCorp Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, Doppler</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Secrets</span></td><td>Storing and rotating API keys and DB passwords</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>AWS KMS, Google Cloud KMS</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Key management</span></td><td>Encryption keys with access control and audit</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Let's Encrypt, AWS ACM</td><td><span class=\"tag\">TLS certificates</span></td><td>Free, auto-renewed HTTPS certificates</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Cloudflare WAF, AWS WAF, OWASP ZAP, Snyk</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Protection & scanning</span></td><td>Block attacks, scan for vulnerabilities in code and dependencies</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Open Policy Agent, Casbin, Oso</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Authorization</span></td><td>Central permission rules across services</td></tr>\n  </table></div></div>\n\n  <div><h3>What breaks, and how we prevent it</h3><div class=\"fails\">\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">SQL injection steals the user table</p><p class=\"fix\">Parameterized queries / ORM, least-privilege DB users, WAF.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">XSS runs attacker JavaScript in users' browsers</p><p class=\"fix\">Escape output, Content Security Policy, httpOnly cookies so scripts can't read tokens.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">CSRF makes a logged-in user submit an action unknowingly</p><p class=\"fix\">SameSite cookies, CSRF tokens, require custom headers for APIs.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">IDOR: changing /orders/41 to /orders/42 shows someone else's order</p><p class=\"fix\">Ownership check on every request; RLS; random non-guessable IDs.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Stolen JWT used for hours</p><p class=\"fix\">15-minute expiry, refresh-token rotation, denylist on logout, bind to device.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">DDoS floods the site</p><p class=\"fix\">CDN/WAF absorbs traffic, rate limiting, autoscaling, anycast.</p></div>\n  </div></div>\n\n  <div><h3>Interview depth</h3><div class=\"qa\">\n    <div class=\"q\"><p class=\"qq\">Authentication vs authorization? <span class=\"lvl\">Beginner</span></p><p>Authentication: who are you (login, token). Authorization: what can you do (roles, ownership checks). 401 means not authenticated; 403 means authenticated but not allowed.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"q\"><p class=\"qq\">Sessions or JWTs? <span class=\"lvl\">Intermediate</span></p><p>Sessions for a single web app: simple and instantly revocable. JWTs when many services or mobile clients need to verify identity without a shared session store. A common hybrid: short JWT access tokens + server-stored refresh tokens that can be revoked.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"q\"><p class=\"qq\">How do you secure service-to-service calls? <span class=\"lvl\">Senior</span></p><p>Zero-trust: mTLS between services (a service mesh issues certificates automatically), each service has its own identity, policies say which service may call which, secrets come from Vault with short leases, and the user's identity propagates in a signed token so downstream services can check ownership.</p></div>\n  </div></div>\n  <p class=\"say\"><b>Say this in the interview:</b> \"Auth through an identity provider issuing short-lived JWTs, verified at the gateway. Services check ownership on every resource. TLS everywhere, secrets in a manager, and cards tokenized by the payment gateway.\"</p>\n</section>\n\n<!-- ============ 12 ============ -->\n<section class=\"topic\" id=\"t12\">\n  <header class=\"th\"><span class=\"num\">12</span><div>\n    <p class=\"eyebrow\">Seeing inside the system</p>\n    <h2>Observability, deployment and operations</h2>\n    <p class=\"lede\">You can't fix what you can't see. Observability is how you know the system is healthy, and safe deployment practices are how you change it without breaking it. Most outages come from deploys, so this matters more than it sounds.</p>\n  </div></header>\n  <p class=\"analogy\"><b>Think of it like</b>a car dashboard. Metrics are the speedometer and fuel gauge (numbers over time). Logs are the trip diary (what happened, line by line). Traces are a GPS track of one journey across every road (one request across every service). Alerts are the warning lights.</p>\n  <figure class=\"dg\"><div class=\"scroll\">\n  <svg viewBox=\"0 0 780 270\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Services send telemetry to an OpenTelemetry collector which routes metrics, logs and traces to their stores, visualized in Grafana and alerting on-call\">\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"10\" y=\"35\" width=\"120\" height=\"44\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"70\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Order svc</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"10\" y=\"110\" width=\"120\" height=\"44\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"70\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Payment svc</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"10\" y=\"185\" width=\"120\" height=\"44\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"70\" y=\"212\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Rider svc</text>\n    <path class=\"e\" d=\"M130,57 L207,120\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/><path class=\"e\" d=\"M130,132 L207,132\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/><path class=\"e\" d=\"M130,207 L207,144\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/>\n    <rect class=\"na\" x=\"210\" y=\"100\" width=\"150\" height=\"64\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"285\" y=\"126\" text-anchor=\"middle\">OpenTelemetry</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"285\" y=\"145\" text-anchor=\"middle\">collector</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"430\" y=\"30\" width=\"180\" height=\"50\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"520\" y=\"52\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Metrics</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"520\" y=\"69\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Prometheus</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"430\" y=\"107\" width=\"180\" height=\"50\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"520\" y=\"129\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Logs</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"520\" y=\"146\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Loki \u00b7 Elasticsearch</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"430\" y=\"184\" width=\"180\" height=\"50\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"520\" y=\"206\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Traces</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"520\" y=\"223\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Jaeger \u00b7 Tempo</text>\n    <path class=\"e\" d=\"M360,122 L427,57\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/><path class=\"e\" d=\"M360,132 L427,132\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/><path class=\"e\" d=\"M360,142 L427,207\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/>\n    <rect class=\"no\" x=\"660\" y=\"80\" width=\"110\" height=\"54\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"715\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Grafana</text><text class=\"tg\" x=\"715\" y=\"121\" text-anchor=\"middle\">dashboards</text>\n    <rect class=\"nw\" x=\"660\" y=\"170\" width=\"110\" height=\"54\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"715\" y=\"194\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Alert</text><text class=\"tb\" x=\"715\" y=\"211\" text-anchor=\"middle\">PagerDuty</text>\n    <path class=\"e\" d=\"M610,57 L657,100\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/><path class=\"e\" d=\"M610,132 L657,110\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/><path class=\"e\" d=\"M610,209 L657,120\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/>\n    <path class=\"eb\" d=\"M715,134 L715,167\" marker-end=\"url(#arb)\"/>\n    <text class=\"ts\" x=\"390\" y=\"262\" text-anchor=\"middle\">A trace_id is created at the gateway and passed in headers through every service, so logs and traces join up.</text>\n  </svg></div>\n  <figcaption>Datadog, New Relic or Grafana Cloud bundle all three stores into one paid product; the open-source stack above is what you'd draw in an interview.</figcaption></figure>\n\n  <div class=\"cols\">\n    <div><h3>What to measure</h3><ul>\n      <li><b>Four golden signals</b> (Google SRE): latency, traffic, errors, saturation.</li>\n      <li><b>RED</b> for services: Rate, Errors, Duration. <b>USE</b> for machines: Utilization, Saturation, Errors.</li>\n      <li><b>Business metrics</b>: orders per minute, payment success rate. A drop here catches bugs that technical metrics miss.</li>\n      <li><b>Structured logs</b> in JSON with trace_id, user_id, request_id, so you can search them.</li>\n      <li><b>Alert on symptoms users feel</b> (error rate &gt; 1%, p99 &gt; 500 ms) based on SLOs, not on every CPU blip.</li>\n    </ul></div>\n    <div><h3>Safe deployment strategies</h3><ul>\n      <li><b>Rolling</b>: replace servers a few at a time. Default in Kubernetes.</li>\n      <li><b>Blue-green</b>: run new version beside old; switch all traffic at once; switch back instantly if bad.</li>\n      <li><b>Canary</b>: send 1% \u2192 10% \u2192 50% \u2192 100% to the new version while watching metrics; auto-rollback on errors.</li>\n      <li><b>Feature flags</b>: ship code switched off, turn it on per user or percentage (LaunchDarkly, Unleash, GrowthBook).</li>\n      <li><b>Database migrations</b>: expand-then-contract (add new column, write both, backfill, switch reads, drop old) so old and new code both work.</li>\n      <li><b>CI/CD</b>: every merge runs tests and deploys automatically (GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, Jenkins, ArgoCD).</li>\n    </ul></div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div><h3>Tools & frameworks</h3><div class=\"tw\"><table>\n    <tr><th>Tool</th><th>Category</th><th>Use it for</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Prometheus + Grafana</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Metrics</span></td><td>Open-source standard for time-series metrics and dashboards</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>ELK (Elasticsearch, Logstash, Kibana), Grafana Loki, CloudWatch Logs</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Logs</span></td><td>Central searchable logs</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>OpenTelemetry, Jaeger, Grafana Tempo, Zipkin</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Tracing</span></td><td>Follow one request across services; OTel is the vendor-neutral standard</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Datadog, New Relic, Dynatrace, Honeycomb</td><td><span class=\"tag\">All-in-one</span></td><td>Paid platforms that do metrics, logs, traces and APM together</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Sentry, Bugsnag</td><td><span class=\"tag\">Error tracking</span></td><td>Frontend and backend exceptions with stack traces</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>PagerDuty, Opsgenie</td><td><span class=\"tag\">On-call</span></td><td>Paging the right engineer, escalation</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>GitHub Actions, ArgoCD, Spinnaker, Argo Rollouts, Flagger</td><td><span class=\"tag\">CI/CD & rollouts</span></td><td>Automated build, deploy, canary analysis</td></tr>\n  </table></div></div>\n\n  <div><h3>What breaks, and how we prevent it</h3><div class=\"fails\">\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Bad deploy breaks checkout for everyone</p><p class=\"fix\">Canary with automated rollback, feature flags, deploy during low traffic, one-click rollback.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Alert fatigue: 200 alerts a day, real one ignored</p><p class=\"fix\">Alert only on SLO burn rate and user-facing symptoms; route the rest to dashboards.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">\"It's slow\" but nobody knows where</p><p class=\"fix\">Distributed tracing shows which hop takes the time; p99 latency per endpoint.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Disk fills with logs and the server dies</p><p class=\"fix\">Log retention policies, ship logs off-box, sample debug logs, alert on disk saturation.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"f\"><p class=\"prob\">Schema change locks a big table during deploy</p><p class=\"fix\">Online migration tools (gh-ost, pg_repack), expand-contract migrations.</p></div>\n  </div></div>\n\n  <div><h3>Interview depth</h3><div class=\"qa\">\n    <div class=\"q\"><p class=\"qq\">Logs vs metrics vs traces? <span class=\"lvl\">Beginner</span></p><p>Metrics are cheap numbers over time that tell you something is wrong. Traces tell you where in the request path it's wrong. Logs tell you exactly what happened at that point.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"q\"><p class=\"qq\">How would you roll out a risky change to the payment flow? <span class=\"lvl\">Intermediate</span></p><p>Behind a feature flag, first to internal users, then a 1% canary watching payment success rate and p99 latency against the old version, automatic rollback if success rate drops by more than a threshold, then ramp up over hours.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"q\"><p class=\"qq\">What are SLOs and error budgets? <span class=\"lvl\">Senior</span></p><p>An SLO like 99.9% successful requests over 30 days allows 0.1% failure (the error budget, ~43 minutes). While budget remains, ship fast. If it's used up, freeze risky launches and invest in reliability. It turns \"how reliable is enough\" into a number both product and engineering agree on.</p></div>\n  </div></div>\n  <p class=\"say\"><b>Say this in the interview:</b> \"I'd instrument everything with OpenTelemetry, track the golden signals plus orders per minute, alert on SLO burn rate, and ship through canary deploys with feature flags.\"</p>\n</section>\n\n<!-- ============ PLAYBOOK ============ -->\n<section class=\"final\" id=\"playbook\">\n  <header class=\"th\"><span class=\"num\">\u25b6</span><div>\n    <p class=\"eyebrow\">Putting it together</p>\n    <h2>The interview playbook</h2>\n    <p class=\"lede\">Every system design question is the same twelve blocks arranged differently. Use this 45-minute structure, and the table below tells you which blocks each famous question leans on.</p>\n  </div></header>\n\n  <div class=\"steps\">\n    <div><b>Requirements \u00b7 5 min</b>Functional: what must it do? (post, follow, see feed). Non-functional: scale, latency, availability vs consistency. Ask, don't assume.</div>\n    <div><b>Estimates \u00b7 3 min</b>DAU, peak QPS, read/write ratio, storage per year. This tells you which blocks you'll need (topic 1).</div>\n    <div><b>API \u00b7 3 min</b>3\u20135 key endpoints with inputs and outputs (topic 2).</div>\n    <div><b>Data model \u00b7 5 min</b>Main entities, which database and why, the shard key (topic 5).</div>\n    <div><b>High-level design \u00b7 10 min</b>Draw client \u2192 CDN \u2192 LB \u2192 services \u2192 cache \u2192 DB \u2192 queue \u2192 workers. Walk one request through it.</div>\n    <div><b>Deep dive \u00b7 15 min</b>Pick the hardest 1\u20132 parts (feed fan-out, seat locking, matching) and go deep. Interviewers often choose; follow their lead.</div>\n    <div><b>Bottlenecks & failures \u00b7 4 min</b>What breaks at 10\u00d7 scale? What if the cache, DB or a region dies? Use the \"What breaks\" cards above.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <figure class=\"dg\"><div class=\"scroll\">\n  <svg viewBox=\"0 0 780 300\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Generic reference architecture combining CDN, load balancer, gateway, services, cache, database, queue, workers, object storage and search\">\n    <text class=\"tt\" x=\"10\" y=\"20\">The reference architecture you'll adapt every time</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"10\" y=\"120\" width=\"90\" height=\"46\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"55\" y=\"147\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Clients</text>\n    <rect class=\"na\" x=\"125\" y=\"45\" width=\"90\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"170\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\">CDN</text>\n    <rect class=\"na\" x=\"125\" y=\"120\" width=\"90\" height=\"46\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"170\" y=\"140\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Load</text><text class=\"t\" x=\"170\" y=\"156\" text-anchor=\"middle\">balancer</text>\n    <rect class=\"na\" x=\"240\" y=\"120\" width=\"100\" height=\"46\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"290\" y=\"140\" text-anchor=\"middle\">API</text><text class=\"t\" x=\"290\" y=\"156\" text-anchor=\"middle\">gateway</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"370\" y=\"60\" width=\"120\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"430\" y=\"85\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Service A</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"370\" y=\"123\" width=\"120\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"430\" y=\"148\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Service B</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"370\" y=\"186\" width=\"120\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"430\" y=\"211\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Service C</text>\n    <rect class=\"na\" x=\"530\" y=\"35\" width=\"110\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"585\" y=\"60\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Redis cache</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"530\" y=\"100\" width=\"110\" height=\"46\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"585\" y=\"120\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Primary DB</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"585\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+ replicas</text>\n    <rect class=\"no\" x=\"530\" y=\"170\" width=\"110\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"585\" y=\"195\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Kafka</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"670\" y=\"170\" width=\"100\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"720\" y=\"195\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Workers</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"670\" y=\"35\" width=\"100\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"720\" y=\"60\" text-anchor=\"middle\">S3</text>\n    <rect class=\"n\" x=\"670\" y=\"100\" width=\"100\" height=\"46\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"t\" x=\"720\" y=\"120\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Search</text><text class=\"ts\" x=\"720\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">index</text>\n    <path class=\"e\" d=\"M100,135 L122,72\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/><path class=\"e\" d=\"M100,143 L122,143\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/><path class=\"e\" d=\"M215,143 L237,143\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/>\n    <path class=\"e\" d=\"M340,138 L367,82\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/><path class=\"e\" d=\"M340,143 L367,143\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/><path class=\"e\" d=\"M340,148 L367,204\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/>\n    <path class=\"e\" d=\"M490,80 L527,57\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/><path class=\"e\" d=\"M490,143 L527,123\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/><path class=\"e\" d=\"M490,206 L527,190\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/>\n    <path class=\"e\" d=\"M640,190 L667,190\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/><path class=\"e\" d=\"M720,170 L720,149\" marker-end=\"url(#ar)\"/><path class=\"nd\" d=\"M670,55 L640,55\"/>\n    <rect class=\"nd\" x=\"240\" y=\"250\" width=\"530\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\"/>\n    <text class=\"ts\" x=\"255\" y=\"275\">Across everything: rate limits \u00b7 timeouts & circuit breakers \u00b7 auth \u00b7 OpenTelemetry \u00b7 multi-AZ</text>\n  </svg></div>\n  <figcaption>For any question, start from this picture, then remove what isn't needed and go deep on what is special about the problem.</figcaption></figure>\n\n  <div><h3>Classic questions \u2192 the blocks that matter</h3><div class=\"tw\"><table>\n    <tr><th>Question</th><th>Key blocks</th><th>The hard part to deep-dive</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>URL shortener</td><td>01 \u00b7 04 \u00b7 05</td><td>Unique short ID generation (base62 of a counter or Snowflake), 100:1 read caching, redirects 301 vs 302</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Rate limiter</td><td>09 \u00b7 04 \u00b7 06</td><td>Token bucket in Redis with atomic Lua, behaviour when Redis is down</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Twitter / Instagram feed</td><td>04 \u00b7 05 \u00b7 07</td><td>Fan-out on write vs read, hybrid for celebrities, feed cache in Redis</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>WhatsApp / chat</td><td>02 \u00b7 05 \u00b7 07 \u00b7 06</td><td>WebSocket connection servers, message ordering, offline delivery, read receipts, sharding by conversation</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Notification system</td><td>07 \u00b7 09</td><td>Fan-out at scale, per-provider rate limits, retries, user preferences, dedupe</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>YouTube / Netflix</td><td>10 \u00b7 04 \u00b7 07</td><td>Upload \u2192 transcode pipeline, adaptive bitrate (HLS), CDN distribution</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Uber / Swiggy delivery</td><td>10 \u00b7 02 \u00b7 06 \u00b7 07</td><td>Geo-indexing drivers, real-time location updates, matching and dispatch, surge pricing</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>BookMyShow / ticket booking</td><td>05 \u00b7 06 \u00b7 09</td><td>Seat locking with expiry, preventing double booking, flash-sale traffic</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>E-commerce checkout / payments</td><td>05 \u00b7 06 \u00b7 07 \u00b7 11</td><td>Idempotency, saga across order/payment/inventory, webhooks, reconciliation</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Google Drive / Dropbox</td><td>10 \u00b7 05 \u00b7 06</td><td>Chunked upload, dedupe, sync conflicts, metadata DB vs blob storage</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Typeahead / autocomplete</td><td>10 \u00b7 04</td><td>Trie / prefix index, offline aggregation, caching per prefix</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Web crawler</td><td>07 \u00b7 10 \u00b7 09</td><td>URL frontier queue, politeness per domain, dedupe with Bloom filters</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Leaderboard</td><td>04 \u00b7 05</td><td>Redis sorted sets, sharding by game, top-K vs rank lookup</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Google Docs</td><td>06 \u00b7 02</td><td>OT or CRDT, WebSocket broadcast, version history</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Metrics / logging platform</td><td>07 \u00b7 05 \u00b7 12</td><td>High write ingestion via Kafka, time-series storage, downsampling, retention</td></tr>\n  </table></div></div>\n\n  <div class=\"cols\">\n    <div><h3>What interviewers score you on</h3><ul>\n      <li>You clarified requirements before drawing.</li>\n      <li>You used numbers to justify choices.</li>\n      <li>Every component has a reason (\"Redis here because reads are 100\u00d7 writes\").</li>\n      <li>You named trade-offs and alternatives, not just one answer.</li>\n      <li>You found the bottleneck and the failure modes yourself.</li>\n      <li>You communicated clearly and checked in with the interviewer.</li>\n    </ul></div>\n    <div><h3>Mistakes that cost offers</h3><ul>\n      <li>Jumping into boxes before understanding the problem.</li>\n      <li>Microservices and Kafka for a problem with 1,000 users.</li>\n      <li>Naming tools without explaining why (\"I'll use Kafka\" and nothing else).</li>\n      <li>Ignoring the single point of failure in your own diagram.</li>\n      <li>Going silent. Think out loud, always.</li>\n      <li>Spending 20 minutes on the easy part and none on the hard part.</li>\n    </ul></div>\n  </div>\n\n  <p class=\"say\"><b>Practice plan:</b> redraw each of the 12 diagrams from memory, then pick one classic question a day, set a 45-minute timer, and run the seven steps out loud. After 15 questions, the patterns repeat and you'll recognize which blocks every new question needs.</p>\n  <footer>System Design Field Manual \u00b7 tools and numbers reflect common industry practice as of 2026; exact limits vary by version and provider.</footer>\n</section>\n</div>";
